          { name: "Rural Healthcare Access & Rural Hospital Viability", tag: "lean-into", why: "A 48.5-year median age constituency is moving into healthcare-intensive life stages, and a district with rural and small-town characteristics cannot assume robust hospital and primary care infrastructure. Rural hospital closures, rural healthcare workforce recruitment, and Medicaid policy are governance issues with direct material impact on Belknap-4 residents' access to emergency and specialty care. The incumbent's legislative record on rural healthcare access and hospital viability is a concrete measure of whether state government is delivering for an aging constituency's real needs." },
          { name: "Demographic Homogeneity & Electoral Invisibility Risk", tag: "careful", why: "The 95.33% white population and 48.5-year median age create a district whose political interests can easily become invisible to state-level political messaging focused on younger, more diverse, or urban constituencies. An incumbent who has allowed Belknap-4 to become a reliably safe seat without active constituent relationship-building risks being caught off-guard if a challenger successfully repositions around issues—workforce development, vocational training, small-business support—that resonate with an aging, homeowner-heavy, working-class constituency. Visibility is not guaranteed by electoral safety; it must be actively maintained." },
          { name: "Assuming Unopposed Victory Reflects Electoral Durability", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed 2024 victory provides no information about whether the incumbent has built a durable constituent coalition or simply has not faced credible opposition. Unopposed elections often occur because opposition has deprioritized a seat—not because the seat is genuinely safe. If a quality challenger emerges in 2026 or 2028 with a message about property taxes, healthcare, or workforce stability, the incumbent will discover that the absence of opposition in 2024 was not evidence of electorate satisfaction but of the opposition's failure to recruit. Complacency based on unopposed victory is the most predictable path to competitive vulnerability." },
          { name: "Over-Relying on College-Educated Voter Messaging in an Under-College-Educated District", tag: "avoid", why: "At 19.4% college attainment—well below state average—Belknap-4's electorate is not primarily composed of college-educated professionals or knowledge-economy workers, and messaging tuned to those constituencies will misfire. Framing centered on higher education access, student debt policy, or diversity and inclusion initiatives resonates in university towns and professional-class suburbs, not in a small-town, homeowner-majority, working-class district where voters evaluate government on property taxes, healthcare access, and whether their skills and experience remain valued in the local economy. An incumbent who has defaulted to college-educated voter messaging while neglecting working-class economic security is vulnerable to a challenger who speaks directly to this district's actual demographic reality." }
