          "Engage college-educated voters on issues beyond tax policy: At 40.9% college attainment, the district's educated majority is attuned to reproductive rights, environmental stewardship, and institutional stability—issues where national Republican positioning may not align with affluent northeastern voters' actual preferences, creating an opening for a credible Democratic challenger to persuade 4-6 percentage points of swing voters.",
          "Identify and engage the 2.2% demographic gap: While small, the gap between reported categories and total population represents real voters whose constituencies and concerns may not be fully visible in standard demographic analysis; determining whether this community is AIAN, immigrant, or other, and what their specific needs are, prevents blind spots in constituent service.",
          "Treat a 74-26 result as the current snapshot, not a structural floor: The absence of younger renters and the aging-in-place dynamic mean this district's Republican margin is held by a very specific demographic cohort—older, affluent, college-educated homeowners—and any challenger who can shift 4-6 percentage points among college-educated women over 55 or persuade younger college graduates to participate at higher rates will find the 26-point margin is more fragile than it appears."
