          { name: "Renter Constituency & Housing Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 43.6% renters in a district with $79,409 median income, NH-HD-Cheshire-2 has a substantial tenant population whose economic security depends on housing affordability and rental market stability. Young professionals, service-sector workers, and families priced out of homeownership represent a real electoral constituency whose needs — rent stabilization, tenant protections, affordable housing development, and livable wages — differ from the homeowner majority. A representative who acknowledges renters' economic constraints and proposes specific policy solutions (zoning reform, rental licensing, wage alignment with cost of living) credibly competes for this 43.6% swing group rather than ceding it to opposition mobilization." },
          { name: "White, Rural-Proximate Demographic Homogeneity & Minority Community Invisibility", tag: "careful", why: "At 90.86% white with 5.04% Hispanic and negligible Black and Asian populations, NH-HD-Cheshire-2 is a demographically homogeneous district where minority communities can easily become invisible in mainstream campaign messaging. The Hispanic population of roughly 500-600 residents in a district of roughly 10,000 is small but present; service-sector workers, agricultural families, and emerging immigrant communities have material needs around language access, workplace protections, and immigration status. Any candidate who runs a campaign entirely framed around white professional and homeowner concerns will miss the opportunity to build genuine relationships with these communities, and will appear to be ignoring their presence." },
          { name: "Treating the 66% Victory as a Durable Floor Rather Than Context-Dependent Alignment", tag: "avoid", why: "The 66-34 Democratic victory in 2024 reflects alignment between Fox's profile, messaging, and the district's college-educated, prosperous professional majority — not an underlying partisan lean that guarantees future Democratic wins. New Hampshire's political environment is volatile at the district level; if economic conditions shift, if housing costs surge further, or if a challenger emerges who more directly addresses renter affordability concerns or younger adult economic anxiety, the current 66% margin could compress rapidly. Any Democratic incumbent or challenger who campaigns this seat as though 66% is a secure baseline is underestimating the fragility of the alignment and missing the opposition's actual vulnerabilities." },
          { name: "Over-Indexing on National Partisan Identity Rather Than Local Governance Performance", tag: "avoid", why: "A district this prosperous, educated, and suburban is capable of voting on local issues — school quality, municipal fiscal management, property tax fairness, zoning and development patterns — independently of national party messaging. Any candidate who campaigns primarily on national issues and partisan identity, rather than demonstrating specific knowledge of Cheshire County governance, local school district performance, and the district's actual development pressures, will underperform relative to a candidate who leads with hyperlocal competence and constituent service. In a 40.9% college-educated district with $79k income, voters expect their representative to understand their municipality's specific challenges, not merely repeat national talking points." }
