          { name: "Property Tax & School Funding Adequacy", tag: "lean-into", why: "The $83,438 median income and 44.9-year median age signal a constituency of established homeowners with school-age children and fixed costs (property tax, education) that are their primary budget concern. Republican messaging in suburban districts increasingly focuses on property tax burden and school choice; a Democratic representative who leads proactively with concrete proposals for property tax fairness and evidence-based school funding is more defensible than one who cedes this issue to the opposition. In a 57-43 seat, ownership of the tax-and-education issue is a prerequisite for holding the margin." },
          { name: "Understanding the 43% Republican Opposition", tag: "careful", why: "At 57-43, nearly half the district's voters chose the Republican candidate; understanding specifically what drives that 43% is critical to holding the seat. In a homogeneous, college-educated suburban district, Republican support is unlikely to be driven by cultural messaging alone but rather by specific frustrations with taxes, school performance, or perceived government inefficiency. Any candidate who assumes the 43% will remain static without understanding their driving concerns is vulnerable to a Republican challenger who addresses those concerns directly." },
          { name: "Treating 57% as a Durable Mandate or a Settled Coalition", tag: "avoid", why: "A 57-43 result in a suburban district is a win but not a comfortable margin in the context of suburban volatility over the past four election cycles. Democratic performance in similar districts has proven sensitive to specific local conditions — school performance changes, municipal service failures, tax increases — and to candidate quality on constituent service. Any incumbent who treats 57% as a floor rather than an outcome to be actively defended through constituent service and local engagement risks losing the seat in the next cycle." },
          { name: "National Partisan Messaging Over Local Accountability", tag: "avoid", why: "A college-educated suburban constituency votes on competence and local delivery first, and national partisan positioning second. Any representative who campaigns or governs primarily through national messaging, partisan positioning, or statewide ideological appeals rather than demonstrating specific competence on schools, taxes, and municipal services is misaligning campaign strategy with actual constituent priorities. In a 57-43 district, this misalignment is sufficient to create challenger opportunity." }
