        issues: [
          { name: "Aging Homeowner Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 55.3 median age and 88.8% homeownership with $64,758 income, NH-HD-Coos-2 is a district of stable but income-constrained older homeowners for whom property taxes, healthcare costs, and rural service affordability are direct monthly budget pressures. The 16.9% college attainment rate indicates limited economic mobility into higher-income brackets, meaning these households are exposed to inflation and fixed-cost increases on essentially static or modestly growing incomes. Legislative responsiveness on property tax fairness, healthcare cost containment, and rural infrastructure investment addresses the material governance concerns that determine whether constituents view their representative as actively serving them or merely holding the seat." },
          { name: "Rural Infrastructure & Service Viability", tag: "lean-into", why: "Coos County's aging population and rural geography create specific infrastructure and service challenges—water and sewer systems, road maintenance, emergency services, and broadband access in dispersed communities—that require state-level policy attention and funding. At $64.8K median income, many households lack the individual resources to absorb infrastructure failure costs, making public investment in these systems a direct quality-of-life issue. A representative who can deliver specific infrastructure projects, coordinate state funding, and solve tangible rural service problems builds constituent relationships that survive political cycles and make the district genuinely difficult terrain for opposition." },
          { name: "Unopposed Status as Electoral Vulnerability Rather Than Strength", tag: "careful", why: "The 100% Republican, unopposed 2024 result is best understood not as a stable majority but as the temporary absence of organized opposition—a fundamentally different phenomenon. In aging rural districts with limited educational attainment and demographic homogeneity, low partisan engagement and potential voter suppression of turnout can mask underlying constituent dissatisfaction or reduced incumbent visibility. An incumbent who treats unopposed status as permission to reduce constituent engagement or service infrastructure creates the conditions for a future challenger to mobilize suppressed opposition or new candidate emergence; the most dangerous path is mistaking absence of opposition for constituent satisfaction." },
          { name: "Treating Demographic Homogeneity as Permission to Ignore Minority Communities", tag: "avoid", why: "At 97.21% white, the temptation is to govern primarily for the majority-majority and treat the 1.74% Latino, 0.11% Black, 0.06% Asian, and ~0.88% likely AIAN residents as negligible. This is both a governance failure and an electoral vulnerability: immigrant and minority communities in rural districts often have specific needs around workplace protections, access to social services, and cultural recognition that go unmet when invisible in constituent outreach, and a future challenger can mobilize these communities around historic neglect. In an unopposed cycle, building relationships with these communities costs nothing and creates a barrier to opposition organizing." },
          { name: "Relying on Partisan Loyalty Rather Than Active Constituent Service", tag: "avoid", why: "A district this homogeneous and this opposed (0% Democratic) can create false confidence that constituent satisfaction is guaranteed or that partisan alignment is sufficient to hold the seat. In reality, rural homeowners at $64.8K income are highly pragmatic voters who evaluate on whether government is solving their problems—property tax fairness, healthcare access, infrastructure maintenance—not on partisan identity alone. An incumbent who operates on the assumption that 97% whiteness and 100% Republican means the seat is safe without active constituent service delivery is positioning themselves for surprise opposition that mobilizes around specific service failures or unmet economic concerns." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "Coos County's Aging, Rural Base: 97% White, $64.8K Income, Median Age 55.3, and an Unopposed Incumbent in a District That Demands Retention Strategy, Not Complacency.",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "NH-HD-Coos-2 is a deeply homogeneous, aging rural district with a median age of 55.3 and a 97.21% white population that places it among the most demographically stable seats in New Hampshire. The district's economic profile—$64,758 median household income, 11.2% renter rate, and only 16.9% college attainment—reflects a homeowner-dominant, working-class to lower-middle-class rural constituency with limited educational credential attainment and stable asset holding in property. The unopposed victory in 2024 and the complete absence of Democratic competition (0% Dem, 100% Rep) might suggest an unassailable seat, but that absence of opposition is itself a strategic vulnerability: unopposed wins in aging rural districts often mask underlying erosion in constituent satisfaction, declining voter turnout, or the potential for a credible challenger to mobilize in a subsequent cycle. The incumbent's task is not to hold an impregnable position—it is to build active constituent engagement that makes the district genuinely difficult territory for a future opponent, rather than merely uncontested in the present.",
          "The district's demographic concentration creates both a strategic asset and a governance blind spot. At 97.21% white, 11.2% renters, and 16.9% college-educated, this is a homogeneous base of stable homeowners with limited economic mobility and high exposure to fixed-cost pressures: healthcare inflation, property tax trajectories, rural service decline, and the viability of aging infrastructure in a region with a median age approaching the state median of 55.3. These are households where medical debt, property tax burden, and the affordability of aging in place are direct material concerns, not abstract policy debates. The demographic homogeneity, however, can create a governance trap: a representative who speaks only to the aging white homeowner majority and neglects the small but present Latino (1.74%), Black (0.11%), and Asian (0.06%) communities—or ignores the governance needs of a 0.88% gap that likely represents American Indian residents—risks both underserving actual constituents and creating an opening for a future challenger to frame the incumbent as unresponsive to the full community. In an unopposed seat, that framing vulnerability is often invisible until a credible opponent surfaces."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Lead with aging homeowner economic security: property tax fairness, healthcare cost management, and rural infrastructure viability are the material concerns of a 55.3-year-old, 88.8% homeowner base at $64.8K income, and specific legislative responses to these concerns build durable constituent trust.",
          "Build active constituent engagement infrastructure despite the unopposed margin: town halls, constituent service, and visible problem-solving in a rural district make the seat genuinely difficult for a future challenger, not merely uncontested today.",
          "Attend to the small but present minority communities (1.74% Latino, 0.11% Black, 0.06% Asian, and ~0.88% likely AIAN): in a district this homogeneous, immigrant and minority constituent needs can become invisible, creating both a governance failure and an opening for an opponent to mobilize these communities around neglect.",
          "Do not assume that 100% Republican and unopposed status creates a stable majority—it indicates absence of organized opposition in this cycle, not a durable constituency relationship; the most dangerous position for an incumbent is mistaking an unopposed win for constituent satisfaction."
        ]
