        issues: [
          { name: "Renter Economic Precarity and Housing Cost Crisis", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 39.4% renting and $43,972 median household income, roughly 15,700 households in this district are spending 35-45% of income on rent — a level that creates month-to-month financial stress and makes housing cost the primary household budget concern. Rental market regulation, tenant protections, and whether state policy is making housing more or less affordable are the direct material issues that drive renter household political behavior. In a district where renters represent over one-third of households and are concentrated in the lower income percentiles, any candidate who addresses housing affordability with specificity will capture swing voters who currently default to opposition but haven't heard a credible plan from the incumbent party." },
          { name: "Aging Workforce Economic Security and Fixed-Income Household Pressure", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 44.3-year median age combined with 14.2% college attainment signals a workforce that has aged in place without credential advancement — people whose wage growth has plateaued and whose retirement security is constrained. Energy costs, property tax burden, and healthcare affordability are the primary financial pressure points for older households at $43,972 income, and messaging about whether state policy is making these costs more manageable commands genuine attention in a district this economically stretched. Candidates who lead with energy cost relief, property tax fairness, and Medicare/healthcare access demonstrate understanding of the material reality of aging workers in rural New Hampshire in ways that abstract tax-cutting rhetoric does not." },
          { name: "Working-Class Cost-of-Living Evaluation", tag: "careful", why: "The 54-46 Republican margin in a district where median income is $43,972 and 39.4% are renters reflects a base that is evaluating whether its economic situation has improved under Republican governance — not inherited partisan loyalty. If economic conditions deteriorate, renter households experience a cost-of-living crisis, or older workers face healthcare or energy cost shocks, the 46% opposition becomes a receptive audience for an alternative message that the 54% base cannot ignore. Any incumbent must actively demonstrate that state policy is delivering material relief rather than assuming the margin is durable." },
          { name: "Treating Low-Education, Low-Income Constituency as Ideologically Motivated", tag: "avoid", why: "The combination of 14.2% college education and $43,972 median income means this district's political behavior is driven by material economic self-interest rather than ideological consistency — yet candidates often campaign as though working-class voters are motivated by abstract principles about size of government or fiscal philosophy. A candidate who campaigns on tax-cutting ideology without addressing renter housing stress or older-worker healthcare costs will find that 46% opposition grows, because the constituency is asking whether it is materially better off, not whether it agrees with conservative principles." },
          { name: "Ignoring Minority Communities' Specific Economic Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "At 5.28% Black and 5.17% Hispanic combined with 10.5% total, these communities represent roughly 4,200 residents whose economic concerns around housing stability, workforce access, and healthcare are distinct from but parallel to the white working-class majority's concerns. Candidates who default to ignoring these communities as demographically small or electorally locked miss both a moral governance obligation and a practical opportunity to demonstrate the breadth of constituent attention required to hold or flip a 54-46 seat. In a district this close, the difference between 54% and losing is captured in whether the minority communities perceive active engagement or benign neglect." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "Coos County House District 5: A Narrow Republican Hold (54-46) in a Working-Class, Older, Low-Education District Where Economic Anxiety and Renter Vulnerability Drive Competitive Dynamics",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "NH-HD-Coos-5 is a deeply working-class district where Republicans won both 2024 seats with a 54-46 margin — a result that appears comfortable but rests on a narrower foundation than the partisan split suggests. The median household income of $43,972 is substantially below state averages, and the 39.4% renter rate is the highest single indicator of economic precarity in the district's profile: these are households with limited asset buffers, exposed to rental market volatility, and vulnerable to cost-of-living shocks in energy, food, and healthcare. The median age of 44.3 and college-educated rate of only 14.2% signal an aging, non-credentialed workforce — people whose career opportunities have narrowed and whose economic security depends on steady, reasonably-waged work that is increasingly difficult to find in rural New Hampshire. The 54-46 Republican margin reflects the homeowner-majority base's preference for tax limitation and fiscal conservatism, but that preference coexists with material economic stress among renters and older workers that is neither captured by the partisan result nor resolved by conservative governance alone.",
          "The district's 85.95% white population, combined with its working-class economic profile and low educational attainment, creates a constituency highly responsive to messaging about job preservation, cost-of-living pressure, and whether government is making life materially harder or easier. The 5.28% Black, 5.17% Hispanic, and small Asian populations are not negligible and deserve specific constituent attention on housing stability and workforce access, but the dominant electoral dynamic is the white working-class household's evaluation of whether its economic situation has improved, stagnated, or declined. With 39.4% renting at $43,972 median income, housing cost as a percentage of household income is likely 35-45% for renters — a level that creates month-to-month financial stress and makes households vulnerable to political messaging about cost-of-living crisis. Any candidate who wants to hold or flip this seat must address the material economic concerns of renters and older fixed-income workers directly: energy costs, property tax burden for homeowners, rental market regulation, and whether local wages are keeping pace with living costs are the substantive questions that will determine whether the 54-46 margin holds or shifts in the next cycle."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Lead with cost-of-living crisis messaging grounded in renter and older-worker economic stress: at 39.4% renting and $43,972 median income, this district is financially stretched, and candidates who acknowledge that reality with specific remedies (rental stabilization, energy cost relief, workforce training) will command credibility with the 46% opposition and consolidate the 54% base.",
          "Avoid abstract fiscal conservatism in favor of targeted economic relief: the district's low education level (14.2% college) and aging workforce mean this constituency evaluates candidates on whether they deliver material improvement in daily costs and job security, not ideological consistency — messaging must be concrete and solution-focused rather than principled.",
          "Engage the 39.4% renter and minority communities (10.5% combined Black and Hispanic) with specific housing and workforce attention: these constituencies are less visible in the partisan margin but represent swing voters who will mobilize if they perceive the incumbent is ignoring their material concerns around rental affordability and economic opportunity.",
          "Recognize that 54-46 is not a stable majority in a district this economically stressed: any candidate who consolidates only the homeowner base and dismisses the renter and working-poor constituencies as lost will find that assumption tested in a recession or if a credible challenger emerges with a genuine cost-of-living message."
        ]
