          { name: "Aging-in-Place Economic Security & Rural Service Infrastructure", tag: "lean-into", why: "With a 53.1-year median age, 91.1% homeownership, and $81,944 income, Grafton-11 is a constituency of older homeowners on fixed or limited incomes whose primary governance concerns are property tax management, healthcare cost sustainability, and the viability of rural infrastructure (broadband, water systems, emergency services) required to age in place. A legislative agenda centered on property tax relief mechanisms, Medicare/Social Security advocacy at the state level, and rural infrastructure investment speaks directly to the material daily concerns of this constituency in ways that abstract fiscal conservatism does not." },
          { name: "Understanding Why Berezhny Was Unopposed & Building Challenger Credibility", tag: "lean-into", why: "An unopposed victory in 2024 could reflect strong incumbency, but it could also reflect Democratic organizational weakness or the perception that this seat is unwinnable—neither of which necessarily means constituents are satisfied with Berezhny's performance on their material concerns. A credible challenger builds legitimacy not by assuming the seat is unwinnable but by identifying specific areas where Berezhny's legislative record has neglected the district's aging-in-place needs and offering concrete alternative proposals on property tax policy, healthcare affordability, or rural service infrastructure that address those gaps." },
          { name: "College-Educated Constituency & Evidence-Based Governance", tag: "careful", why: "At 30.8% college attainment—above state average—Grafton-11 has a constituency that is more likely to demand specific policy detail and evidence-based claims than generic partisan messaging. Both incumbent and challenger need to ground their arguments in concrete data about property tax burden, healthcare cost trends, and rural service metrics rather than ideology; a college-educated audience will evaluate on whether the numbers actually support the claim, not whether the claim fits the partisan narrative." },
          { name: "Treating an Unopposed Outcome as Proof of Stability", tag: "avoid", why: "Zero opposition in a heavily Republican district can signal strong incumbency, but it can equally signal that no credible challenger has emerged or that Democratic recruitment has written off the seat—neither of which is proof that constituents are satisfied with current representation. An incumbent who assumes zero opposition means permanent electoral security is vulnerable to the first challenger who demonstrates specific, evidence-based alternatives on the aging-in-place issues that actually matter to this constituency." },
          { name: "Assuming Inherited Republican Loyalty Rather Than Pragmatic Constituent Evaluation", tag: "avoid", why: "College-educated, older, asset-holding homeowners are not ideological voters—they are pragmatists who evaluate based on whether government is delivering outcomes on the issues that affect their quality of life. A representative who campaigns as though this constituency is bound by Republican identity rather than engaging them on their specific material concerns (property tax fairness, healthcare cost control, rural infrastructure) misreads the actual basis of this constituency's voting behavior and leaves openings for a challenger with more concrete proposals." }
