          { name: "Property Tax Burden & Fiscal Management", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $126,804 median income and 88.1% homeownership, Hillsborough-1 residents experience property tax as a direct, annual household cost of thousands of dollars — not an abstract principle. The 65-35 Republican sweep suggests this constituency judged Democratic candidates as unable or unwilling to manage tax burden responsibly. Any candidate seeking to win or hold must offer specific, credible fiscal policy: property tax relief mechanisms, revenue efficiency analysis, or structural tax reform grounded in this district's actual tax rates and burden trajectory, not generic anti-tax rhetoric." },
          { name: "Education Investment & School Quality", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 37.7% college-educated adults and high median age suggesting households with school-age children and college-savings stakes, this constituency cares deeply about public education quality and funding — but within a fiscal constraint framework (property tax burden limits school funding options). The 35% Democratic vote likely includes professionals prioritizing education investment; a winning strategy requires demonstrating that school quality and fiscal discipline are not mutually exclusive, with concrete evidence of cost-effective school management." },
          { name: "Economic Anxiety Among High-Income Households", tag: "careful", why: "Affluent households at $126k income are not immune to inflation impact, interest rate effects on mortgages and business lending, or recession risk — their economic anxiety is real and material, but expressed through concern about governance competence, fiscal discipline, and whether state policy creates headwinds or tailwinds for business and employment stability. The 65% result may partly reflect high-income households concluding that Democratic fiscal policy creates unnecessary economic drag; understanding whether this concern persists or has stabilized is critical to competitive positioning in 2026." },
          { name: "Treating the 65% as a Durable Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district this affluent and educated, swing voting and candidate-specific evaluation are normal rather than exceptional — a 65-35 result is a specific moment's judgment on fiscal management and economic stewardship, not a stable baseline. Any incumbent Republican who campaigns as though the margin is durable without continuous demonstration of fiscal discipline and competence will find it compressed by a credible Democratic challenger offering a coherent alternative on education or economic management." },
          { name: "Cultural or Ideological Framing", tag: "avoid", why: "Hillsborough-1 is not a district moved by culture-war positioning or abstract ideological frames — it is a district of educated, affluent professionals who evaluate on concrete governance outcomes and fiscal management. Candidates who rely on cultural wedge issues or partisan identity signaling rather than specific policy answers to property tax, education funding, or economic management are leaving their case incomplete and vulnerable to an opponent who offers genuine fiscal or education policy substance." }
