        issues: [
          { name: "Affluent Suburban Professional Governance Expectations", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $160,253 median income and 61.4% college attainment, Hillsborough-2 is a constituency of educated professionals with high expectations for competent, fiscally responsible governance. In a 52-48 Republican district, these voters are genuinely persuadable based on candidate quality and whether they perceive serious policy engagement on property taxation, education funding, healthcare access, and municipal fiscal management. Candidates who demonstrate deep knowledge of education policy, healthcare cost drivers, and property tax alternatives win support in this constituency; those who default to partisan positioning lose swing voters to more substantive opponents." },
          { name: "At-Large District Structure & Ticket-Splitting Voters", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 7-seat at-large structure means all winners emerge from the same voter pool, and success requires finishing in the top seven across the full district — not simply consolidating a partisan base. In a 52-48 Republican district with 61.4% college attainment, meaningful numbers of affluent professionals split tickets and vote for individual candidates rather than parties. Candidates who build cross-party appeal, demonstrate institutional credibility, and speak to moderate swing voters' concerns will outperform those targeting only their partisan base in the final ranking." },
          { name: "Asian Professional Community & Swing Potential", tag: "careful", why: "The 8.11% Asian population in an affluent suburban professional district likely overrepresents technology, healthcare, finance, and education professionals — constituencies that evaluate strongly on economic policy competence, education quality, and institutional stability. In a 52-48 Republican district, Asian voters represent a meaningful swing constituency whose support can shift based on candidate positioning on healthcare costs, education funding, and economic opportunity. Candidates who engage this community's specific concerns — rather than treating Asian voters as a monolithic bloc — strengthen their coalition position in an at-large race." },
          { name: "Partisan Ideological Positioning Over Governance Substance", tag: "avoid", why: "A 61.4% college-educated, $160k-income district at 52% Republican will punish candidates who lead with abstract ideological claims or culture-war positioning at the expense of substantive policy engagement. Affluent suburban professionals in this district evaluate on competence and whether governance delivers tangible results for their community — not on whether a candidate demonstrates partisan purity. Candidates perceived as ideologically rigid or disconnected from material governance concerns will underperform in the at-large ranking relative to opponents seen as serious and substantive." },
          { name: "Taking the 52% Republican Margin as a Safe Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "A 52-48 split in a district this educated and affluent is structurally vulnerable to persuadable swing voters moving based on candidate quality and perceived competence. In an at-large race, the difference between finishing first and finishing eighth is often a matter of a few thousand votes across a coalition of ticket-splitters and swing professionals. Any candidate who campaigns as though 52% represents a comfortable majority will lose persuadable voters to opponents perceived as more serious and more attuned to the constituency's governance expectations." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "NH-HD-Hillsborough-2: A Highly Educated, Affluent At-Large District at 52% Republican Where Suburban Professionals and Moderate Voters Control the Outcome",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "Hillsborough-2 is among New Hampshire's most affluent and educated districts: $160,253 median household income, 61.4% college attainment, and a 44.4-year median age that positions it as a suburban professional community with substantial economic security and civic engagement. The 52-48 Republican margin in a district this educated and affluent is the critical strategic fact — a narrow 4-point spread in a community that should, on demographic grounds, lean more decisively Republican. This gap reflects a constituency of college-educated professionals and homeowners (83.4% ownership implied by 16.6% renter rate) who evaluate candidates on competence, institutional stability, and whether governance serves their material interests, not on partisan tribal loyalty. The 7-seat at-large structure means all seven winners emerge from the same voter pool: a candidate must finish in the top seven vote-getters across the entire district, which rewards candidates who can build coalition support across party lines and appeal to swing voters in this affluent suburban middle.",
          "The demographic composition — 82.95% white, 8.11% Asian, 4.33% Hispanic, 0.58% Black — reflects a homogeneous suburban professional community with meaningful Asian representation (likely technology, healthcare, and finance professionals) but limited racial diversity. The 61.4% college attainment rate is the dominant strategic signal: this is a constituency of educated professionals whose voting behavior is driven by policy competence, institutional trust, and whether they perceive a candidate as serious and substantive rather than ideologically rigid. In a district at 52% Republican with an at-large seven-seat structure, the actual swing constituency is college-educated suburban professionals — many of them ticket-splitters — who are genuinely persuadable based on candidate quality, issue specificity, and whether they trust a candidate to govern responsibly. A candidate who wins here must speak to this constituency's actual concerns: property tax fairness, education quality, healthcare access and costs, and whether local governance is delivering competent stewardship of their community. Generic partisan positioning will underperform in a district this educated and this close."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Lead with competence and institutional stewardship: in a 61.4% college-educated district at 52% Republican, affluent suburban professionals evaluate candidates on policy specificity and whether they can demonstrate serious governance capability, not on partisan activation. Candidates who lead with abstract ideology or culture-war positioning will lose persuadable swing voters to opponents perceived as more substantive.",
          "Understand that the 48% Democratic share reflects real persuadable swing constituency: in a district this educated and affluent, meaningful numbers of Republican-lean voters split tickets and evaluate based on individual candidate quality. Building support among ticket-splitting college professionals is the path to finishing in the top seven in an at-large race.",
          "Address affluent suburban professional concerns with specificity: property tax management, education funding and quality, healthcare cost transparency, and municipal fiscal responsibility are the material governance issues for a $160k-income constituency. Candidates who speak to these concerns concretely outperform those relying on partisan rhetoric.",
          "Recognize the Asian demographic (8.11%) as a meaningful and persuadable swing constituency: in a suburban professional district, Asian voters — likely overrepresented among technology, healthcare, and finance professionals — evaluate strongly on economic policy, education quality, and institutional competence. Targeted engagement with this community builds coalition strength in an at-large race."
        ]
