        issues: [
          { name: "Renter-Majority Housing & Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 52.6% renters — the highest concentration in this analysis — Hillsborough-6 has an economically vulnerable constituency for whom housing cost burden, tenant protections, and affordable housing development are direct material concerns. With a median household income of $75,793 and a significant renter population, this district's households are spending a disproportionate share of income on housing in an expensive regional market. Any candidate who wants to hold or win this seat must address housing affordability and renter-focused economic security with specificity, not abstractions." },
          { name: "Hispanic Community Integration & Economic Opportunity", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 18.01% Hispanic/Latino, Hillsborough-6 has the largest Hispanic population of any district analyzed here and a constituency whose economic mobility, workplace safety, healthcare access, and immigration status concerns directly shape their electoral behavior. The 56-44 Democratic sweep suggests that Hispanic voter mobilization or candidate preference is a significant swing factor in this district's competitive dynamics. A winning candidate must engage the Hispanic community with concrete policy specificity on employment, education, and healthcare access — not as a separate constituency, but as part of a unified economic security message." },
          { name: "College-Educated Urban Constituency & Education Policy", tag: "careful", why: "At 38.3% college attainment and a median age of 44.2, Hillsborough-6 has a more educated, urban-leaning constituency than many New Hampshire districts. This population's concerns around public education funding, higher education affordability, and quality-of-life urban services are real and present. However, the 56-44 Democratic margin suggests that college-educated voters are not unified — a significant minority (44%) remain persuadable or already aligned with Republican messaging on fiscal or governance grounds. A candidate must engage this constituency authentically on education issues without assuming their support is automatic." },
          { name: "Treating the Democratic Sweep as a Stable Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "The 2024 Democratic sweep of all three Hillsborough County House seats at 56-44 is significant but not a landslide — 44% of voters chose Republican representation, and in a swing-capable constituency with a large renter population, meaningful Hispanic representation, and an educated but divided electorate, that 44% cannot be written off as a permanent minority. Any Democratic incumbent who campaigns as though 56% represents a stable majority rather than a working coalition of renters, college-educated voters, and Hispanic households will risk losing seats when one of these constituencies shifts or disengages." },
          { name: "Assuming Homogeneous Urban Progressive Consensus", tag: "avoid", why: "Nashua is a mid-sized New Hampshire city with real economic and cultural diversity — renters and homeowners, college-educated professionals and working-class families, a significant Hispanic population alongside the white majority at 70%. Any candidate who campaigns as though this district is an urban progressive monoculture will miss the actual coalition-building required to win and hold the seat. The 52.6% renter rate and 18.01% Hispanic population create specific constituencies whose concerns are not fully captured in a generic 'urban progressive' frame." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "Nashua's Renter-Majority, Hispanic-Plurality District: 52.6% Renters and 18% Hispanic in a 56-44 Democratic Seat Held by Specific Coalition Management, Not Inherited Partisan Alignment",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "Hillsborough-6 is New Hampshire's most economically and demographically diverse House district — a working-class urban constituency centered on Nashua where the median household income of $75,793 masks significant within-district variation between college-educated professionals and renters with constrained economic mobility. At 52.6% renters, this is the only district in this analysis where renters outnumber homeowners, and that renter supermajority transforms the politics of housing, landlord regulation, and cost-of-living issues from peripheral to central. The 2024 Democratic sweep at 56-44 reflects consolidation of renters, college-educated voters (38.3% college attainment), and the district's 18.01% Hispanic population — the largest Hispanic share in any district analyzed here — into a working majority. However, this is a coalition, not a mandate; the 44% Republican opposition is substantial and includes both homeowners and persuadable renters with conservative fiscal concerns.",
          "The most important strategic fact about Hillsborough-6 is that Democrats won by coalition-building, not by demographic inevitability — and holding the seat requires understanding which voters within the coalition are most at risk of defection or disengagement. The renter population's primary concerns are housing affordability, tenant protections, and cost-of-living pressure in an expensive regional market; they are not automatically Democratic. The Hispanic community at 18% has demonstrated significant mobility in New Hampshire House races, and their support is contingent on specific engagement on workplace issues, education, and healthcare access — not symbolic acknowledgment. The college-educated constituency at 38.3% has split political views; a meaningful minority support Republican candidates on fiscal and governance grounds. A winning candidate in Hillsborough-6 must actively serve all three constituencies with policy specificity, recognizing that the 56% result is durable only if it reflects genuine constituent alignment, not inherited partisan loyalty."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Lead with renter economic security and housing affordability: at 52.6% renters with median household income of $75,793, this district's housing cost burden is acute and is the primary economic concern for the supermajority constituency — tenant protections, affordable housing development, and rent stabilization are the issues that move renters' electoral behavior.",
          "Engage the Hispanic community with workplace specificity: at 18.01%, Hispanic voters are the district's largest minority and a core part of the Democratic majority — but their support is earned through concrete attention to employment conditions, workplace safety, education access, and healthcare, not through generic diversity messaging.",
          "Recognize the college-educated constituency as persuadable: at 38.3% college attainment, this is an educated electorate, but the 44% Republican share proves that college education does not predict Democratic support — fiscal responsibility, government effectiveness, and quality public services are the issues that drive this constituency's electoral decisions.",
          "Treat 56% as a working coalition, not a majority: the Democratic sweep at 56-44 is real but not insurmountable; any incumbent or challenger who assumes renter voters, Hispanic voters, or college-educated professionals will remain locked into their 2024 alignment without active constituent service will risk losing the seat in a persuasion-capable electorate."
        ]
