          { name: "Healthcare Access and Coverage Equity", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 22.83% Hispanic population in a northern New England district likely includes both documented and undocumented households, and both face significant barriers to healthcare access — coverage gaps, language barriers in provider networks, dental and vision care gaps, and rural health access constraints in the exurban portions of Hillsborough County. With a $92,148 median income that may not reflect lower-income Hispanic households' actual earnings, healthcare access is a direct and acute concern that affects multiple dimensions of family stability and economic mobility. A candidate who demonstrates fluency on coverage expansion, language access requirements, and provider network equity in the Hispanic community is offering concrete constituent service, not generic Democratic healthcare positioning." },
          { name: "Hispanic Community Political Participation and Representation", tag: "careful", why: "At 22.83%, the Hispanic population is large enough to swing a three-seat at-large race if mobilization or candidate preference shifts significantly, yet it is often treated as automatically Democratic without active constituent relationship-building. The community's presence in the lower-income and renter segments of the district means its economic concerns (housing, wages, healthcare) may not align perfectly with the college-educated homeowner base's priorities, and candidates who assume automatic Hispanic Democratic loyalty rather than actively earning it through specific policy engagement risk suppressing mobilization or losing a portion of the community to low-turnout. This requires authentic engagement with Hispanic community organizations, demonstrated fluency on issues affecting the community specifically, and willingness to prioritize resource allocation toward Hispanic constituent service." },
          { name: "Treating the 74% Democratic Margin as a Safe Baseline", tag: "avoid", why: "A three-seat at-large format with a 74% Democratic result means 2 of 3 seats are expected to be Democratic, but that expectation is contingent on actual candidate performance, voter turnout distribution, and whether Democratic candidates consolidate both the college-educated base and the working-class and renter plurality. The 74% reflects the current coalition, not a structural floor — if Hispanic turnout declines, or if working-class renter households perceive that neither party is addressing their concerns, the margin narrows and the third seat becomes genuinely competitive. Any candidate who campaigns on the assumption that 74% is a safe hold rather than a coalition that must be actively maintained is mathematically overconfident." },
          { name: "Assuming Upper-Middle-Class Income Profile Reflects Uniform District Experience", tag: "avoid", why: "The $92,148 median household income creates an optical illusion of prosperity that masks the 35% renter population and the likely lower-income concentration among Hispanic households. Candidates who lead with upper-middle-class concerns (capital gains taxation, Ivy League admission equity, investment strategy) rather than the material economic security concerns of renters and working-class families are misaligning their message with the actual composition of the district. The aggregate income figure is real, but it reflects homeowner wealth and dual-income professional households that are not representative of the district's renter, immigrant, and working-class plurality." }
