          { name: "Education Quality & School Funding", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 41.1% college attainment — among the highest in New Hampshire — and a $109,296 median income, this constituency views education as both a public value and an economic asset directly affecting property values and family opportunity. The at-large 2-seat structure means both seats will be scrutinized on education policy specifically. State education funding levels, local control over curriculum, the state's education rankings relative to regional competitors, and the quality of teachers and facilities are governance questions where this constituency will evaluate candidates concretely. A candidate who cannot articulate a specific vision for education excellence and fiscal sustainability will struggle to consolidate support in a district where education matters to household economics and identity." },
          { name: "Fiscal Policy & Tax Fairness Among Affluent Homeowners", tag: "careful", why: "At $109,296 median income with 79.7% homeownership, this constituency experiences property taxes and state income/wage taxation as direct household costs that affect after-tax wealth accumulation. The 20.3% renter rate is low, meaning renters are a meaningful but minority constituency. Tax policy disagreements within affluent communities often center on the distribution of tax burden between property owners and wage earners, the fairness of tax rates relative to services received, and the responsiveness of government to tax revenue — issues where college-educated voters evaluate on principle and self-interest simultaneously. Any candidate must be prepared to explain tax philosophy with specificity to a constituency that can do the math themselves; vague commitments to 'fiscal responsibility' will not move votes in a district this financially literate." },
          { name: "Assuming High Income Guarantees Conservative Voting Across All Issues", tag: "avoid", why: "Affluent, college-educated voters often diverge from conservative orthodoxy on environmental regulation, social policy, and the role of government in providing public goods — and the split result in a 66% Republican district is direct evidence that this divergence exists in Hillsborough-16. Any candidate who assumes that a $109,296 median income voter will automatically support deregulation, tax cuts, or cultural conservative positions without addressing the specific policy merits is misreading this constituency's actual values and priorities." },
          { name: "Treating the Seat as Safely Held by Partisan Numbers Alone", tag: "avoid", why: "The 1D-1R result in a district where Republicans outnumber Democrats by two-to-one is powerful evidence that partisan registration does not predict the at-large outcome — candidate quality, local governance competence, and policy positions matter more. Any incumbent or challenger who campaigns this district assuming that 66% Republican registration guarantees two Republican seats, or who relies on partisan machinery without building a personal case for election, is ignoring the most important fact the 2024 result revealed: this district elects its seats on merit and policy, not on party loyalty." }
