          { name: "Homeowner & Higher-Income Republican Coalition", tag: "careful", why: "The 37% Republican result in a district that is 74% white and contains a homeowner minority within the renter majority represents a real, defensible coalition of people who believe Republican economic policy — tax policy, regulation, and business incentives — serves their interests better than Democratic alternatives. This constituency is not a default Republican base; they are active political participants who evaluate candidates on whether Republican positions on their material concerns are credible. Any winning candidate must either consolidate this coalition (if Republican) or draw substantial defection from it (if Democratic), and doing either requires engaging their economic concerns seriously, not dismissing them as obstacles." },
          { name: "Treating 63% as a Safe Democratic Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "The 63-37 split reflects genuine ideological division in the district, not a stable Democratic majority. At 53.5% renters and $60,405 median income, the Democratic base is economically precarious and evaluates candidates on concrete delivery — not inherited party loyalty. A Democratic candidate who campaigns as though 63% is a safe mandate, rather than a coalition that must be actively maintained through credible economic policy, risks low turnout and swing defection. The 37% Republican constituency is real, organized, and will mobilize in the next cycle if the current representative's economic record disappoints renters." },
          { name: "Assuming 2-Seat At-Large Structure Protects an Incumbent", tag: "avoid", why: "In a two-seat at-large race, both candidates finish in top two to win — meaning the district's 63-37 split creates genuine competition for both seats. An incumbent assuming that the 2-seat structure provides safety, or that their party's partisan margin is sufficient protection, misreads how at-large races function. Both candidates must compete actively for the top-two finish, and in a district this divided, the outcome depends on turnout, persuasion, and which coalition is more energized. Any candidate who campaigns defensively, rather than actively competing for persuadable swing voters and base turnout, risks finishing third and losing." }
