          { name: "Hispanic Community Engagement & Economic Opportunity", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 17.64% Hispanic population is large enough to be a genuine swing constituency in a perfectly split 50-50 district — their turnout patterns and candidate preference choices are mathematically sufficient to determine which party takes both seats. Immigration policy, economic mobility, access to education and healthcare, and whether they have authentic representation in state government are the issues that activate Hispanic voter participation in Hillsborough. A candidate who builds a genuine relationship with the Hispanic community — not token acknowledgment, but specific policy engagement on their economic and civic concerns — can shift the district's balance decisively." },
          { name: "Understanding the 50-50 Split as Strategic Instability", tag: "careful", why: "A 50-50 result in a two-seat at-large district means both parties have real constituency, but it also means neither party has a stable baseline or a floor beneath which they cannot fall. The 50% results for both parties reflect current coalition choices, not demographic inevitability, and any shift in turnout or candidate preference among renters, the Hispanic community, or younger adults can swing both seats. Any candidate who campaigns as though their party's seat is secure in a 50-50 district is strategically blind to the real electoral vulnerability they face." },
          { name: "Treating the Hispanic Community as a Monolithic Voting Block", tag: "avoid", why: "While the 17.64% Hispanic population is large enough to be electorally significant in a 50-50 district, treating it as a single constituency with uniform concerns glosses over the diversity within the Hispanic community — immigration status, national origin, economic sector, and tenure in New Hampshire all create different perspectives on policy priorities. A candidate who defaults to generic Hispanic outreach rather than engaging specific community organizations, labor groups, and neighborhood associations will find that engagement superficial and their candidate support soft." },
          { name: "Ignoring the Homeowner Majority's Fiscal Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "The homeowner constituency in Hillsborough is real and substantial, and they are evaluating representation on property tax fairness, municipal service quality, and neighborhood stability — not on abstract ideological positioning. A candidate who campaigns toward renters and younger adults while neglecting homeowner fiscal concerns will lose homeowner support to a challenger who takes their material interests seriously, which is particularly dangerous in a 50-50 district where every constituency group matters to the final outcome." }
