        memoHeadline: "Hillsborough-20: A Perfectly Split 50-50 District with a 35.8% Renter Constituency, Significant Hispanic Population (17.64%), and No Partisan Baseline — Every Seat Is Genuinely Contested",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "NH-HD-20 is one of the most electorally volatile districts in the state: a perfect 50-50 split between Democratic and Republican seats in 2024 signals a district with no inherent partisan lean and no safe baseline for either party. The demographic profile explains why — this is not a monolithic constituency. At 21.6% college-educated adults and a $72,719 median household income, the district sits at the state median but contains sharp internal divisions: 35.8% renters versus homeowners suggests a constituency with divergent economic interests and housing security concerns. The 17.64% Hispanic population is substantial and strategically significant in a swing district where turnout and coalition choices among that community can swing both seats. The 66.33% white plurality, 12.34% Black, and 5.45% Asian populations together create a genuinely diverse district where no single demographic group can be taken for granted. A 50-50 result means both parties have real constituency, real grievances, and real reasons to show up — and the representative who wants to win or hold this seat must understand what each community needs and why they have reason to doubt either party will deliver.",
          "The 39.1-year median age and the combination of renters and homeowners create a constituency evaluating representation through competing lenses: younger renters and households at housing cost constraints are likely evaluating on affordability, economic mobility, and whether government is making housing more or less accessible; middle-aged and older homeowners are evaluating on property tax fairness, service quality, and whether their neighborhood is stable. The Hispanic community at 17.64% is large enough to be a genuine swing constituency in a 50-50 district — their turnout and candidate preference choices are mathematically sufficient to determine which party takes the second seat. The absence of a clear partisan majority means the candidate who wins here is the one who builds a coalition around specific material concerns: housing affordability for renters, tax fairness for homeowners, economic opportunity for young adults, and genuine engagement with the Hispanic community on immigration policy, economic mobility, and whether they have a voice in state-level decisions that affect them. A 50-50 result is not a floor — it is a warning that any candidate who consolidates only their existing coalition and ignores the opposition's legitimate concerns will lose the next cycle to a challenger who takes those concerns seriously."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Build a renter-focused economic agenda: at 35.8% renters with a $72,719 median household income, housing affordability, rental regulation, and the cost of living in Hillsborough are direct constituent concerns that swing district voters evaluate on material outcomes, not partisan label.",
          "Engage the Hispanic community (17.64%) with specificity on immigration policy, economic opportunity, and whether they have a voice in state government — in a 50-50 district, their turnout and candidate preference is the difference between winning and losing both seats.",
          "Consolidate homeowner support on property tax fairness and neighborhood stability: the homeowner majority needs to believe their representative is protecting their asset and their community, which requires concrete fiscal policy and not abstract ideology.",
          "Treat a 50-50 result as mathematically unstable: any candidate who campaigns as though their party's current seat is secure will lose to a challenger who takes the opposition's concerns seriously and offers concrete answers to why they should switch."
        ]
