          { name: "College-Educated, High-Income Professional Constituency", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 59.3% college attainment and $124,485 median household income, NH-HD-Hillsborough-21 is among the most educated and affluent districts in New Hampshire — a constituency that votes on policy substance, institutional competence, and alignment with professional-class values. The 2024 Democratic sweep at 70% reflects this cohort's current preference, but high-income, college-educated voters are genuinely persuadable on education quality, tax policy, and fiscal management when candidates present credible alternatives. Republicans cannot win this seat by ignoring the professional majority; they must engage it directly on the specific governance issues that matter to households at this income and education level." },
          { name: "Suburban Family Stability & Schools", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 37.1-year median age, 74.7% homeownership rate, and $124,485 income profile a constituency of established families prioritizing school quality, property values, and neighborhood stability. In a two-seat at-large district where both seats went Democratic at 70%, the winning coalition included suburban parents evaluating education funding, school governance, and whether the state is investing in the districts' institutions. Any candidate seeking to flip or hold this seat must lead with concrete education policy — funding mechanisms, curriculum rigor, special education support — and demonstrate how their legislative record or platform improves schools without destabilizing the fiscal foundation on which these homeowners depend." },
          { name: "Renter Minority & Housing Cost Pressure", tag: "careful", why: "The 25.3% renter rate, while below the state average, still represents roughly one-quarter of the district's households who experience the economy differently than the homeowner majority — facing rising rents in a market where median household income is $124,485 but starter rents may consume 35-40% of income. This constituency was likely key to the Democratic 70% result; young professionals, service workers, and families priced out of ownership are responsive to housing policy, rental protections, and zoning reform. Republicans need not cede renters entirely, but cannot win without at least holding them to high single-digit support — which requires demonstrating how supply-side housing policy (zoning reform, development incentives) serves renters' long-term interests in affordability." },
          { name: "Treating the 70% Result as Stable Without Understanding It", tag: "avoid", why: "The Democratic 70% sweep of both seats in 2024 is a strong result, but does not mean the district is locked in Democratic hands — 30% of voters preferred Republicans, and that 30% likely includes the homeowner core that can be mobilized around education quality, property tax management, and fiscal conservatism. Democrats risk assuming that affluent, educated constituencies stay aligned by default; Republicans risk assuming that because they lost 70-30, the district is unwinnable. The actual strategic reality is that this is a high-income, highly-educated, persuadable constituency that votes on governance competence and policy substance — not party loyalty. Either party that takes this district for granted will find it more competitive than the 40-point margin suggests." },
          { name: "Identity-Based or Ideological Messaging in a Substance-Focused District", tag: "avoid", why: "With 59.3% college education and $124,485 income, Hillsborough-21 is a constituency accustomed to evaluating policy on merits and expects candidates to engage issues with intellectual rigor. Generic culture-war messaging, identity-based appeals, or ideological positioning without substantive policy content will be read as evasion by a constituency that evaluates candidates on competence and specificity. Republicans particularly need to avoid the impression that they are running on grievance rather than governance; Democrats need to avoid the impression that they take this affluent, educated base for granted and can win on turnout alone without substantive education and fiscal policy platforms." }
