          { name: "Taking a 68-32 Margin as a Safe Seat", tag: "avoid", why: "A 36-point Democratic margin is robust against Republican challenge in the near term, but it masks the actual economic precarity of the constituency and the conditionality of Democratic voting behavior. Renters and lower-income households vote Democratic when they perceive Democratic governance as addressing their material concerns; complacency about the margin, insufficient constituent service, or economic conditions that worsen despite Democratic control can rapidly erode base enthusiasm." },
          { name: "Ignoring Black & Asian Communities as Distinct Constituencies", tag: "avoid", why: "At 5.79% Black and 2.39% Asian, these communities represent meaningful portions of the district whose specific economic and social policy concerns can become invisible when Democratic incumbents default to generic 'diversity' framing or absorb these communities into broader minority narratives. Each community has distinct economic mobility, healthcare access, and social policy concerns that deserve specific legislative attention and constituent service." }
