        issues: [
          { name: "Education Funding and School Quality", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 44.1% college attainment and $115,833 median household income, Hillsborough-28 is a constituency of college-educated professionals with school-age children and strong incentives to ensure educational quality. Education funding adequacy, teacher compensation, and school infrastructure investment are direct quality-of-life concerns, not abstract policy debates. A representative who can demonstrate concrete delivery on education funding — particularly in a state where education has been a persistent political tension — commands credibility with the district's largest demographic asset." },
          { name: "Healthcare Cost and Medicare Sustainability", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 47.3 median age, a significant portion of Hillsborough-28's voters are approaching or navigating Medicare eligibility, and older homeowners on fixed incomes face rising healthcare costs as a direct financial pressure. Prescription drug pricing, rural hospital viability, and the long-term solvency of Medicare are material economic security issues, not ideological debates. Any candidate who addresses these concerns with specificity and legislative record demonstrates understanding of the district's actual governing priorities." },
          { name: "Understanding the 38% Opposition as an Active Competitive Threshold", tag: "careful", why: "In a 2-seat at-large format, 38% opposition is not marginal — it is a coalition mathematically capable of winning one seat if turnout increases or candidate preference shifts by four to five percentage points. The opposition is distributed across college-educated professionals, older voters concerned about healthcare costs, and potentially younger households priced out of the district's high cost of living. Any incumbent treating 38% as a stable minority rather than an active threat underestimates the vulnerability of the at-large format; any challenger underestimates what it takes to persuade affluent, college-educated homeowners to flip." },
          { name: "Generic Tax-Cutting Messaging", tag: "avoid", why: "A constituency at $115,833 income with 44.1% college attainment and strong education funding expectations does not respond to abstract tax-cutting rhetoric — they respond to evidence that government is delivering quality education, infrastructure, and healthcare access. Tax fairness and efficient spending matter more than tax minimization; any candidate leading with 'cut taxes' rather than 'invest in education and manage fiscal responsibility' is misreading the constituency's actual priorities." },
          { name: "Assuming Partisan Loyalty Among College-Educated Professionals", tag: "avoid", why: "College-educated professionals in affluent districts are not reliably partisan voters — they evaluate on competence, specific policy delivery, and alignment with their economic interests, which are complex. A Republican assuming college-educated homeowners will vote Republican regardless of education funding outcomes, or a Democrat assuming college-educated voters are automatically Democratic, is ignoring the actual basis on which this constituency makes electoral decisions. Credibility and demonstrated competence matter more than party affiliation in a district this educated and affluent." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "Hillsborough-28: A Wealthy, Aging, College-Educated Republican Stronghold Where the 38% Opposition Signals Emerging Vulnerability in a 2-Seat At-Large Format.",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "Hillsborough-28 is demographically one of New Hampshire's most affluent and educationally advanced districts: $115,833 median household income, 44.1% college attainment, 47.3 median age, and 94.1% non-Hispanic white population with only 5.9% renters. These are the characteristics of an established, asset-holding, professionally employed constituency with strong material incentives toward economic conservatism and minimal pressure from housing insecurity. The 62-38 Republican sweep in 2024 reflects this base alignment — but the critical strategic fact is that 38% of the district voted against the Republican sweep in a 2-seat at-large race where both Republicans won. In a two-seat format, 38% opposition is not marginal; it represents a coalition of Democratic voters large enough that a single strong Democratic candidate could finish in the top two if turnout or preference shifts by four to five percentage points. The Republicans hold both seats because they consolidated their base; they do not hold them because they are numerically dominant.",
          "The demographic profile suggests multiple layers of potential opposition: college-educated professionals (44.1%) who may diverge from Republican positions on education funding, healthcare access, or social issues; older homeowners (47.3 median age, 94.1% ownership implied by 5.9% renters) whose fixed incomes and healthcare costs create vulnerability to messaging around prescription drug pricing and Medicare sustainability; and a constituency wealthy enough to have geographic mobility and quality-of-life priorities that may not align with partisan orthodoxy on environmental regulation, infrastructure investment, or education adequacy. The $115,833 income is high enough that property tax management and education funding quality — not tax cutting as an abstract principle — are the operative concerns. Any Republican defending these seats must consolidate affluent homeowner support through concrete delivery on education, infrastructure, and fiscal management; any Democrat seeking to break through must address why affluent, college-educated professionals should prefer Democratic governance on the specific issues that matter to their material security and their children's futures. The 38% is not a ceiling — it is an active coalition waiting for a candidate who speaks credibly to their priorities."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Understand that 38% opposition in a 2-seat at-large race is not a minority — it is a competitive threshold; if Democratic turnout increases or if a credible Democratic candidate can persuade college-educated and older voters that the Republican incumbent is not delivering on education, infrastructure, or healthcare issues, the math allows for one Republican seat to flip.",
          "Lead with education adequacy and property tax fairness: a constituency at $115,833 income and 44.1% college attainment cares about whether schools are well-funded and whether the tax burden is structured fairly, not whether state spending is minimized — offer concrete education and infrastructure investment rather than tax-cutting rhetoric.",
          "Address healthcare cost concerns directly: at 47.3 median age with implied high homeownership, voters are evaluating Medicare sustainability, prescription drug pricing, and rural hospital viability as personal economic security issues, not abstract policy debates — demonstrate specific legislative action on these concerns.",
          "Do not assume college-educated professional voters: at 44.1% college attainment, this is a highly educated constituency skeptical of partisan positioning unsupported by evidence; any candidate seeking to hold or win this district must lead with competence, specificity, and track record rather than ideology or rhetoric."
        ]
