        issues: [
          { name: "Understanding the 2024 Rightward Shift", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 57-43 Republican result in a prosperous, educated, older homeowner district is not a natural baseline — it represents a meaningful shift that requires identification and consolidation. Property tax anxiety, school funding concerns, state-spending frustration, or specific local governance decisions most likely drove this movement. Any candidate who does not identify and actively address what moved voters in 2024 will inherit a margin that looks stable but is actually fragile, vulnerable to a challenger who re-engages the issues that actually matter to this constituency." },
          { name: "Property Tax and Fixed-Income Homeowner Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 46.9 median age, $93,208 income, and 92.4% homeownership, Merrimack-5 is a district of older homeowners for whom property tax burden, municipal service efficiency, and the long-term affordability of staying in their homes are direct, material concerns. Rising property tax rates, school funding pressures, or municipal spending increases directly threaten the economic security of a constituency with substantial home equity but semi-fixed incomes. Legislation or governance positions that take property tax management and municipal efficiency seriously as primary issues — not as secondary afterthoughts to state-level priorities — activate and consolidate this constituency's support." },
          { name: "Education Policy and College-Educated Skepticism", tag: "careful", why: "The 28.6% college-attainment rate is notably lower than New Hampshire statewide averages, suggesting a constituency educated enough to value education but potentially skeptical of system costs, credentialism, or institutional inefficiency. The demographic gap may indicate blue-collar professionals, tradespeople, and working families who have thrived without college credentials. A candidate who offers a coherent education vision that balances broad access with fiscal responsibility, and acknowledges both the value and the limits of traditional credentialing pathways, addresses a real tension in this district's values that a purely pro-institution stance will miss." },
          { name: "Treating 57% as a Durable Majority Rather Than a Realignment", tag: "avoid", why: "A 57-43 result in a previously competitive district is not a natural floor — it is a specific outcome driven by specific 2024 conditions, voter concerns, and candidate positioning. Any incumbent or challenger who assumes the 57% represents a stable coalition without actively understanding and consolidating the issues that drove 2024 will find the margin erodes when new candidates run, different issues dominate, or opposition research surfaces governance vulnerabilities. This district swung right recently; it can swing again if no one is actively maintaining the connection between the Republican platform and the specific concerns that moved voters." },
          { name: "Dismissing or Minimizing the 43% Opposition Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "In a high-income, educated, older district, the 43% Democratic share is not a marginal or fringe constituency — it likely includes college-educated professionals, working families, longtime community members, and voters with substantive governance concerns that differ from the current majority. A candidate who campaigns as though 57% represents consensus, dismisses opposition concerns as ideological rather than rooted in material interests, or treats the 43% as opponents rather than fellow constituents to be engaged will miss opportunities to expand the coalition and will face a credible challenger who takes those voters' concerns seriously." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "Merrimack-5: A High-Income, Aging Homeowner District That Just Turned Hard Republican — Understand What Flipped and Consolidate It.",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "Merrimack-5 is a prosperous, stable, older homeowner community with a median household income of $93,208 — among New Hampshire's highest — and a median age of 46.9 that signals a constituency with substantial home equity and fixed-income concerns. The 7.6% renter rate confirms this is an asset-holding, property-owner-dominant district where home values, property taxes, and long-term economic stability are the primary material concerns. The 2024 result — a decisive 57-43 Republican sweep of both seats — represents a significant shift in a district that has historically been competitive in New Hampshire statewide politics. This is not a naturally red district that simply came home to Republicans; it is a district that chose to move substantially rightward in 2024, and understanding what drove that movement is the single most important analytical task for any candidate seeking to win or hold these seats.",
          "The demographic composition — 95.29% white, 28.6% college-educated, 46.9 median age, and $93,208 income — describes an educated, affluent, older, and homogeneous constituency that is present in high concentrations across suburban New Hampshire but is not itself an automatic partisan indicator. What matters here is why a district with this profile swung from competitive to 57-43 Republican in a single cycle. The most probable drivers are some combination of property tax anxiety among older homeowners on fixed or semi-fixed incomes; concerns about state spending or education funding efficiency; frustration with Democratic governance at the state level; or specific local issues tied to Merrimack city governance or regional development decisions. The 28.6% college-attainment rate is notably lower than New Hampshire's statewide average, which may indicate a constituency skeptical of credentialing elites or progressive institutional claims. Any candidate who assumes the 57-43 margin is durable without identifying and actively consolidating the specific concerns that drove 2024 will find the seat vulnerable when the next election cycle brings different salience priorities or a credible challenger who addresses what actually moved voters."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Identify the specific 2024 drivers: Conduct granular research on property tax trends, school funding debates, local spending decisions, or state policy shifts that moved Merrimack-5 from competitive to 57-43 Republican — and build the legislative agenda around consolidating those winning issues rather than assuming the margin will hold on partisan momentum alone.",
          "Lead with property tax and fixed-income homeowner security: At 46.9 median age, 93.2k income, and 92.4% homeownership, this is a constituency where property tax burden, municipal service delivery, and long-term housing cost management are direct quality-of-life concerns — frame these as governance priorities, not secondary issues.",
          "Engage the college-educated plurality with specificity on education policy: The 28.6% college-attainment rate and $93k income suggest a constituency that values education but may be skeptical of system inefficiency or cost escalation — offering a specific, credible vision of education funding that balances access with fiscal responsibility addresses this swing constituency directly.",
          "Treat the 43% opposition as a real constituency with real concerns: In a high-income, educated, older district, the Democratic 43% is not a fringe group but likely includes college-educated professionals, working families, and community-rooted voters with legitimate governance concerns — a winning campaign must acknowledge and address their priorities specifically, not run as though the 57% represents immutable consensus."
        ]
