        issues: [
          { name: "Affluent Homeowner Economic Security & Property Tax Fairness", tag: "lean-into", why: "With a median household income of $95,307, a 72.4% homeownership rate, and a 43.0-year median age, Merrimack-12 is a constituency of asset-holding professionals and families whose primary fiscal concerns are property tax management, education funding (which drives property value), and whether state tax policy is fair to homeowners. The narrow 53-47 margin suggests that affluent Republicans in this district are not locked in; they expect governance that delivers measurable fiscal stewardship and transparent property tax allocation. Republicans who lead with property tax fairness, education funding for competitive schools, and fiscal discipline command the language of this district's economic self-interest." },
          { name: "College-Educated Professional Constituency & Workforce Development", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 30.2% college attainment and $95,307 median income, Merrimack-12 is dominated by educated professionals and their families whose economic interests center on professional-economy viability, healthcare access, and workforce pipeline sustainability. The Democratic 47% in a district this educated is not an anomaly — it reflects educated voters whose policy priorities around healthcare, environmental protection, and economic inclusion may diverge from conservative positioning. Candidates who engage this constituency on substantive education, workforce development, and healthcare policy specifics (not ideology) demonstrate respect for the district's actual population and build credibility for converting swing voters." },
          { name: "Younger Adults & Non-Homeowning Residents' Housing & Economic Opportunity", tag: "careful", why: "At 27.6% renters and 43.0 median age, Merrimack-12 includes a meaningful cohort of working-age adults and young professionals who rent rather than own and whose material economic concerns — housing affordability in a high-income district, student debt, professional opportunity — may be invisible to homeowner-focused political messaging. In a two-seat at-large contest where turnout among younger and non-homeowning voters can shift the outcome, any candidate who assumes this cohort will vote with their parents' generational interests is strategically vulnerable. Understanding what younger professionals and renters in a prosperous district actually need — and whether either party is offering it — is essential to holding or flipping at-large seats." },
          { name: "Partisan Identity as the Primary Electoral Driver", tag: "avoid", why: "A 53-47 result in a district this affluent and educated is not driven by inherited partisan loyalty but by substantive policy disagreement and evaluation of whether the incumbent agenda serves the district's actual interests. Candidates who campaign primarily on partisan identity (conservative versus liberal, Republican versus Democrat) rather than on concrete policy outcomes — property tax fairness, education funding, healthcare access, professional-economy viability — are conceding the terms of engagement to their opposition and suggesting they lack specific answers to the constituency's material concerns." },
          { name: "Treating Educated Voters as a Captive Demographic", tag: "avoid", why: "With 30.2% college attainment and income above the state average, Merrimack-12's educated professionals vote based on substantive policy alignment and evidence that government is delivering results, not on assumption of partisan affinity. A candidate who campaigns as though college-educated voters in a prosperous district 'should' vote for their party without demonstrating specific policy responsiveness to education, healthcare, economic opportunity, or fiscal stewardship is strategically failing to compete for a meaningful share of this district's actual electorate. College-educated professionals in affluent districts are the most persuadable swing constituency in New Hampshire politics." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "NH-HD-Merrimack-12: A Affluent, Aging, Education-Conscious Homeowner District Where Republicans Hold a Narrow 53-47 Edge and Must Defend Against Demographic Drift.",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "NH-HD-Merrimack-12 is a prosperous, overwhelmingly white (96.07%), college-educated (30.2%), and homeowner-dominant district in central New Hampshire with a median household income of $95,307 and a median age of 43.0 years. The 2024 Republican sweep of both at-large seats with a 53-47 margin represents a genuine win in absolute terms but obscures a strategic vulnerability: this district's demographic profile — high income, high education attainment, 72.4% homeownership, aging population — should produce a more durable Republican margin in a state where Republicans hold statewide office. The 47% Democratic share, combined with the district's affluence and education level, suggests a constituency that is not voting against Republicans out of economic desperation but rather out of substantive policy disagreement, cultural orientation, or dissatisfaction with the incumbent governing agenda. In a district where 30.2% college attainment is above the state average and income is well into the top quartile, Democratic voters are not a fringe minority but a meaningful plurality of the community's most educationally and economically successful residents.",
          "The narrow 53-47 margin in a district this affluent and educated demands strategic recognition that the current Republican hold is contingent, not structural. At 43.0 median age with 27.6% renters, Merrimack-12 includes a meaningful cohort of younger adults and non-homeowning residents whose economic concerns — housing costs, student debt, healthcare access in a changing economy — may diverge from the homeowner-majority agenda. The 1.42% non-white population gap (the remaining ~1.3% of the district after accounting for reported categories) suggests negligible demographic cushion for Republican consolidation; any increase in turnout from younger, college-educated, or non-homeowning residents shifts the competitive math. Republicans in this district cannot rely on inherited partisanship or demographic inevitability — they must win on substantive grounds by demonstrating that their agenda delivers material results for affluent homeowners, educated professionals, and the working-age adults who live here. Democrats have a genuine opening if they can articulate an economic and cultural vision that resonates with college-educated professionals in a prosperous district and mobilize the 47% into active participation in a two-seat at-large contest."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Recognize that 53-47 in an affluent, educated district represents a narrow plurality, not a mandate; Republican incumbents must defend on substantive policy grounds (property tax fairness, education funding, healthcare access) rather than assuming demographic advantage will hold.",
          "Mobilize younger adults and non-homeowning residents (27.6% of the district) around economic security: housing affordability, student debt, and whether local wages support family formation in a high-income area where many younger professionals still struggle with cost of living.",
          "Engage the college-educated plurality (30.2%) directly on education funding, workforce development, and whether state policy supports the professional economy that drives Merrimack-12's prosperity — this constituency is not taking their support for granted and will reward specificity.",
          "Target the 47% opposition for conversion rather than dismissal: understand whether Democratic voters are concentrated among younger professionals, renters, educated women, or suburban professionals with policy-specific concerns, and offer concrete answers rather than partisan rhetoric."
        ]
