          { name: "Housing Affordability and Renter Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 37.1% renter rate in a district with $70,841 median household income, housing affordability is a material economic constraint for more than one-third of constituents. Renters in suburban New Hampshire face rising rents, limited inventory, and compressed wage growth — the core economic pressure on this demographic. Any candidate who does not address this constituency's need for renter protections, affordable housing development, and tenant stability will find them responsive to a challenger who does; this is not an ideological issue but a direct household budget question." },
          { name: "School Funding and Property Tax Fairness", tag: "lean-into", why: "The homeowner majority (roughly 63%) at median age 41.8 includes families with school-age children and retirees on fixed incomes — two populations with direct stakes in education funding and property tax policy. School quality and tax burden are the primary household economic concerns for a suburban homeowner base; a representative who takes these issues seriously and can demonstrate concrete results on either dimension commands the credibility required to hold this constituency at current margin levels." },
          { name: "College-Educated Constituency Engagement on Policy Substance", tag: "careful", why: "The 31.8% college-educated share is substantially above average and represents a constituency that evaluates on policy knowledge, evidence, and track record rather than partisan loyalty. This group is capable of supporting either party if persuaded that the candidate understands their priorities and can execute on them; a representative who relies on partisan messaging or abstract positioning rather than substantive policy engagement risks losing credibility with the college-educated plurality, particularly on education, environmental governance, and fiscal transparency." },
          { name: "Framing Housing Affordability as a Renter vs. Homeowner Zero-Sum Issue", tag: "avoid", why: "The most dangerous strategic error is to position renter affordability and homeowner property tax fairness as competing interests requiring candidates to choose one or the other. In a district with 37.1% renters and 63% homeowners at $70,841 income, both constituencies have legitimate economic concerns and both are voting; a candidate who frames their interests as fundamentally opposed will alienate whichever group they deprioritize. The winning message integrates both: sustainable housing supply and affordability that supports both renter access and neighborhood quality for existing homeowners." },
          { name: "Taking the 56-44 Margin for Granted as a Durable Base", tag: "avoid", why: "A 12-point margin in a college-educated, mixed-tenure suburban district where 44% of voters chose the opposition is not a safe majority — it is a working plurality that requires active constituent engagement to maintain. An incumbent who assumes the 56% will hold simply by consolidating existing supporters, without continuing to address the material concerns of both renters and homeowners, will find the margin contract sharply in the next cycle; this is a seat that demands continued attention to the coalition that won it, not complacency about the result." }
