        issues: [
          { name: "Educated Voter Political Fragmentation", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 28.3% college attainment rate in a district with 95% white, $137,813 median income, and 79% homeownership should produce a 70%+ Republican margin — but the actual result is 64-36. The 4-6 point gap between expected and actual Republican performance in a demographic this favorable points to political fragmentation among educated, prosperous voters on non-economic grounds: climate policy, reproductive rights, or institutional competence concerns are likely activating Democratic support among highly educated affluent households. A candidate must understand whether this represents a soft opposition that can be persuaded back to Republicans through substantive engagement, or a durable political preference among educated voters for Democratic governance despite economic self-interest." },
          { name: "Homeowner Tax Policy & Community Investment", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 83.3% homeownership and $137,813 median income, property tax burden and school funding directly affect the primary constituent interest. This is not an anti-tax constituency — it is a constituency that expects tax dollars to produce measurable community quality: well-funded schools, maintained infrastructure, and reliable public services. Messaging that frames lower taxes as an end in itself will underperform; messaging that positions tax fairness and strategic public investment in education and community infrastructure as interconnected will resonate with affluent homeowners who have no interest in degraded public goods." },
          { name: "Understanding Opposition Coalition Stability", tag: "careful", why: "The 36% Democratic share in a district this affluent, educated, and white could represent either a ceiling of Democratic support (meaning the Republican majority is durable) or a floor that can be expanded through targeted outreach to educated voters on specific policy concerns (meaning the seat is more competitive than the 64% suggests). The district's demographic profile alone cannot answer this question — a candidate must actively understand which voters in the opposition are potentially persuadable, which are committed partisans, and whether any discrete policy positions or governance failures are driving the 36% higher than baseline expectations." },
          { name: "Anti-Affluence Messaging or Class Resentment Politics", tag: "avoid", why: "A district where the median household income is $137,813, homeownership is 83%, and the constituency is 95% white is fundamentally hostile to messaging that positions affluence or homeownership as a problem. Attempts to appeal to economic grievance or class-based resentment politics will be received as inauthentic or insulting in a district where most voters are prosperous and see their economic position as the result of work and sound decisions. Any candidate who frames this constituency's interests as illegitimate or requiring moral correction will alienate the primary electorate." },
          { name: "Treating 64% as a Durable Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district this affluent, educated, and homeowner-dominant, the 64% Republican result should be 70%+ — the 6-point gap represents a real deviation from demographic expectation that any candidate must take seriously rather than dismiss as normal variance. Treating the 64-36 result as a stable, self-sustaining Republican majority without understanding why educated affluent voters are voting Democratic at rates 6 points higher than demographic fundamentals predict will blind a candidate to genuine vulnerabilities on specific policy or governance issues that educated voters prioritize." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "Rockingham-9: A Wealthy, Highly Educated, 95% White District at 64% Republican — the Strategic Question Is Not Why Republicans Won, But Why One-Third of This Affluent Constituency Voted Democratic.",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "NH-HD-Rockingham-9 is a two-seat at-large district where Republicans swept both seats with 64% of the vote in 2024 — a decisive margin in New Hampshire's competitive political environment. The district's demographic profile is distinctive and economically secure: $137,813 median household income (among the highest in the state), 28.3% college attainment, 38.9 median age, 95.28% white, and only 16.7% renter rate. These are the material characteristics of a prosperous suburban and small-town constituency where homeownership is the norm, educational attainment is above state average, and household finances are stable relative to regional and national benchmarks. On its surface, these numbers suggest a naturally Republican constituency — and the 64-36 result confirms that such a constituency does exist. But the strategic fact that dominates this district is not the Republican victory; it is the 36% Democratic share in a district where every demographic indicator points toward Republican comfort.",
          "The 36% Democratic result in Rockingham-9 cannot be explained by demographic distress, educational polarization, or the economic anxiety that typically drives Democratic gains in affluent areas. Instead, it points to one of two possibilities — or both operating simultaneously. First, there may be genuine political fragmentation among highly educated, prosperous homeowners on cultural or governance issues that have nothing to do with economic self-interest: climate policy, reproductive rights, institutional trust, or local governance competence may be driving college-educated affluent voters away from Republicans despite their economic alignment with conservative tax policy. Second, the 36% may represent a ceiling of Democratic support in a district this wealthy and white, not a floor — meaning that the Republican margin is genuinely stable and the opposition represents a consistent but contained constituency. Any candidate seeking to hold or win this seat must understand which dynamic is actually operating. If it is the former, Republicans in Rockingham-9 are vulnerable to educated affluent voters on non-economic grounds and must address those concerns directly. If it is the latter, the Republican majority is durable but requires continued attention to the governance and institutional issues that keep educated voters engaged. Neither interpretation permits the assumption that a 64-36 result in a demographic this favorable is self-sustaining.",
          ""
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Diagnose the 36% opposition specifically: in a district this affluent and educated, the Democratic third is not driven by economic hardship but by discrete political positions on cultural issues, climate policy, or institutional trust — identify which issues are most salient to educated voters in Rockingham-9 and either address them directly or build a credible counter-narrative rather than assuming demographic alignment is sufficient.",
          "Lead on education and institutional competence: the 28.3% college attainment rate and high median income create a constituency that evaluates representatives on policy knowledge, substantive governance, and evidence-based reasoning — generic conservative messaging will underperform in a district this educated.",
          "Protect the homeowner majority and property tax messaging: with only 16.7% renters and $137,813 median income concentrated among homeowners, tax policy directly affects the primary constituent interest — but frame this around fairness and investment in community quality rather than abstract anti-tax ideology.",
          "Build durable engagement with the educated electorate rather than assuming it: affluent, college-educated voters can swing between parties based on institutional and cultural factors — treat this constituency as requiring ongoing constituent dialogue, not passive coalition management."
        ]
