          { name: "Housing Affordability & Cost-of-Living Governance", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 55.9% renter rate in an affluent district ($115,417 income) is anomalous and signals real housing cost pressure even among high-income professionals; this is a genuine vulnerability for Democratic incumbents if they are perceived as accepting housing cost escalation without action. Young professionals, established renters, and families entering the housing market in Rockingham County face material pressure around housing costs, and Democratic messaging that acknowledges these costs with concrete policy responses—zoning reform, housing development support, property tax fairness—is essential to holding the coalition. Ceding housing affordability to Republicans as a 'free market' issue means surrendering a material governance concern to opposition messaging." },
          { name: "Regional Economic Transition & Professional-Sector Viability", tag: "careful", why: "Rockingham County is in structural economic transition from manufacturing and traditional employment toward professional services and knowledge work; the district's high college attainment and income reflect this shift, but the transition is not complete or universally benefiting all residents. A Democratic incumbent should monitor whether older workers, small-business owners, or service-sector workers displaced by regional economic change are beginning to realign toward Republican messaging about regulation, taxation, or government efficiency. If the transition narrative shifts from 'opportunity for professionals' to 'decline for traditional workers,' the 74% coalition could fracture around economic anxiety rather than professional-class alignment." },
          { name: "Treating 74% as a Safe Supermajority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 74% result in a high-income, highly-educated district is not a safe baseline but a reflection of current alignment between the incumbent's governance record and the constituency's material interests; if governance fails on education, housing, or environmental stewardship, the same professional-class sophistication that produced 74% Democratic support can organize an alternative. A challenger who campaigns as a pro-business, education-quality, housing-reform moderate could potentially crack this coalition if the incumbent is perceived as inattentive to material governance delivery." },
          { name: "Assuming the 26% Republican Opposition is Irrelevant", tag: "avoid", why: "The 26% Republican vote in a 70.8% college-educated, $115k-income district represents a meaningful constituency of business owners, property-tax-conscious homeowners, and older professionals whose material interests diverge from the Democratic coalition; these voters are neither ignorant nor fringe, but rather constituents whose priorities around regulation, property tax, or business environment genuinely differ. Any Democratic incumbent who dismisses this 26% or treats them as beyond reach forfeits the opportunity to expand or stabilize the coalition by engaging their material concerns around property tax fairness or business competitiveness." }
