        memoHeadline: "Rockingham-30: An Affluent, Aging Homeowner District at 63% Republican—Vulnerable to Candidate-Specific Erosion Among College-Educated Voters and the Silent Swing of the Missing 5.7% Population.",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "NH-HD-Rockingham-30 is a comfortable but not commanding Republican seat in a district where demography suggests deeper partisan stability than the current 63-37 margin reflects. With a median household income of $87,021, median age of 53.2 years, and 89.46% white population, this is a constituency of older, asset-holding homeowners in a relatively prosperous corner of Rockingham County—precisely the demographic profile that should yield a solid Republican baseline. Yet the 63-37 result, while clearly favoring Republicans, leaves 37% of the district actively supporting Democrats in a state where the statewide Republican margin is substantially larger. The district's 26.1% college attainment rate is the critical variable: this is above the New Hampshire average and represents a constituency of educated professionals, business owners, and knowledge workers whose economic interests and social attitudes may diverge from the straightforward homeowner conservatism that the raw partisan number implies. A representative who assumes that 63% is a stable floor rather than a contingent coalition is not reading the district's actual composition.",
          "The missing 5.7% of the population—the gap between the four reported Census categories (89.46% + 3.58% + 0.76% + 0.72% = 94.52%) and 100%—almost certainly represents American Indian / Alaska Native residents and multiracial households that the standard Census reporting obscures. While this gap is smaller in absolute terms than in rural western or southwestern districts, in a district this prosperous and educated, it likely represents an even more economically integrated and politically active constituency than raw demographic percentages suggest. More important, the 28.2% renter rate in a district with this income level signals the presence of younger professional renters, service-sector workers, and fixed-income retirees who may not appear prominently in the homeowner-dominant political narrative but who represent real constituent concerns around housing affordability, healthcare access, and whether government is delivering economic opportunity. The 63-37 margin obscures a district where aging homeowners hold the plurality, but not the whole story—and where specific candidate positioning on education funding, healthcare costs for older adults, and economic opportunity for younger professionals can shift outcomes meaningfully.",
          "The two-seat at-large structure creates a mathematical incentive for both parties to run slates, but it also creates opportunity for candidate-specific performance: a voter who splits their ballot or votes for fewer than two candidates can reshape the outcome. In a 63-37 district, the Republican path to both seats is straightforward if candidates consolidate the homeowner base and hold even modest support among college-educated professionals. The Democratic path requires either dramatically increasing turnout among the 37% or persuading a meaningful subset of college-educated Republicans and younger professionals that their specific interests—education quality, property tax fairness, healthcare costs—are being neglected. The incumbents' task is not to expand beyond the current coalition, but to ensure that no credible alternative emerges that speaks more directly to the actual economic concerns of the 26.1% college-educated constituency and the 28.2% of renters who may feel that their interests are being subordinated to a narrower homeowner-only agenda."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Consolidate the aging homeowner base by leading on property tax management, municipal service quality, and healthcare cost control for Medicare-age constituents—but recognize this is 53% of the district, not 100%, and assume nothing about their children or younger professionals who rent.",
          "Engage college-educated professionals (26.1%) on education funding quality, professional workforce development, and whether the district's schools and community colleges are competitive—this constituency is economically successful enough to be persuadable, but only if a candidate demonstrates specific knowledge of their concerns.",
          "Do not cede the renter and younger professional constituency (28.2% renters in a district with $87K median income) to Democrats; these are often Republican-lean voters in New Hampshire who evaluate on economic opportunity and government competence, not ideology, and they need to hear a specific case for why Republican governance serves their interests.",
          "Treat 63% as a working majority vulnerable to candidate-specific erosion, not a durable coalition—any incumbent who assumes the seat is safe because of favorable demographics, without actively managing the college-educated and younger professional portion of the electorate, is creating an opening for a credible Democratic challenger to perform above the district baseline."
        ]
