          { name: "Renter Community & Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 41.6% renter rate in NH-HD-Strafford-8 is substantially higher than typical suburban districts and signals a constituency segment that is economically distinct from the homeowner majority. Renters at this income level face direct pressure from housing cost inflation, and their electoral behavior is responsive to candidates who address affordability and economic mobility specifically. At 41.6% of the district, renters are not a marginal population — they represent a genuine swing constituency that neither party can ignore. Republicans must articulate why their economic platform delivers for renters; Democrats can build competitive advantage here by centering housing affordability and wage policy in ways that resonate with younger and more economically precarious households." },
          { name: "The 45% Opposition in a Suburban Context", tag: "careful", why: "Farrington's 55% win in a district with 90.14% white population and 58.4% homeownership represents a suburban Republican coalition that should be producing larger margins in New Hampshire's political landscape. The 45% opposition cannot be explained by demographic minorities alone — the Hispanic, Black, and Asian communities total only 3.55% of the population. The 45% opposition therefore reflects either persuadable homeowners with specific governance grievances, the renter community's economic concerns translating to electoral behavior, or a combination of both. Any incumbent or challenger who does not understand specifically what generated that 45% — whether it centers on education funding, healthcare costs, local tax policy, or something else — is governing without a full strategic picture. The opposition's concentration and composition is the actionable intelligence that determines whether 55% is durable or vulnerable." },
          { name: "Treating College-Educated Adults as Politically Monolithic", tag: "avoid", why: "NH-HD-Strafford-8's 29.6% college attainment rate is lower than many suburban New Hampshire districts, and the temptation to assume educated adults vote a particular way is strategically dangerous in a district where college-educated voters are less than one-third of the population. The homeowner majority and $72K median income suggest a working-class-to-middle-class professional base that is economically conservative but may have specific local governance concerns. Treating college-educated adults as a monolithic progressive constituency, or assuming they will vote based on cultural or national partisan messaging rather than local delivery, will miss the actual persuasion opportunity in a district where most voters lack a college degree and prioritize economic performance." },
          { name: "Racial Demographic Framing as the Primary Electoral Driver", tag: "avoid", why: "With 90.14% white population, racial diversity politics are not the primary lever in this district — and attempting to activate electoral behavior through racial framing, either defensively or offensively, is strategically misdirected. The actual swing constituency is renters and homeowners responding to economic concerns, local governance delivery, and cost-of-living pressures. A candidate who centers racial or cultural messaging in a district where 90% of voters are white and 41.6% face direct housing affordability pressure is ignoring the material concerns that actually move voters here. The election is won or lost on whether Strafford-8 residents believe their representative is delivering on property taxes, education funding, healthcare access, and housing — not on abstract cultural positioning." }
