        issues: [
          { name: "Housing Affordability & Young Professional Economic Mobility", tag: "lean-into", why: "In a district where 34.8% are renters, median age is 20.7 years, and income is $126,658 — well above state median but concentrated among young professionals — housing cost-to-income ratios are the primary constraint on the pathway from renting to ownership. Zoning reform, housing supply expansion, and anti-speculation policy are the mechanisms by which state government enables or obstructs this transition. A candidate who demonstrates concrete legislative delivery on housing supply rather than abstract positioning commands the economic credibility this constituency demands." },
          { name: "Student Debt & Healthcare Access for College-Educated Young Adults", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 77.8% college-educated adults, this district has a constituency with direct lived experience of education costs and healthcare affordability constraints during the early-career phase. Student loan forgiveness frameworks, healthcare affordability for young adults not yet on employer plans, and the alignment of state education funding to workforce needs are concrete governance concerns that affect material quality of life. A candidate who engages these issues with specificity rather than general rhetoric demonstrates understanding of the actual economic position of the majority of the constituency." },
          { name: "Non-Traditional Household Formation & Civic Engagement Patterns", tag: "careful", why: "The 20.7-year median age and 34.8% renter rate signal a constituency where single young professionals, early-career couples, and student-connected households are the dominant demographic pattern rather than traditional families. This population has different civic engagement modes, housing needs, healthcare patterns, and policy priorities than traditional family-centered constituencies, but campaigns often default to family-centered messaging. Recognizing and responding to the actual household formation patterns in the district is necessary to build authentic connection with this constituency, not a secondary accommodation." },
          { name: "Treating the 89% Margin as a Safe Floor Rather Than a Ceiling", tag: "avoid", why: "An 89% result in a 4-seat at-large district is not a safe majority — it is an aggregate margin that masks underlying coalition composition and specific candidate performance. In a district with 77.8% college-educated adults and strong ticket-splitting behavior patterns among young professionals, a candidate who consolidates only the partisan base and neglects specific engagement on housing, education debt, and healthcare affordability can underperform relative to the district's lean and risk losing a top-4 finish. The 89% is the floor outcome when candidates actively serve the constituency's material concerns, not an automatic return." },
          { name: "Assuming Party Loyalty Among a Highly Educated, Mobile Young Professional Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "Young professionals in high-education, high-income districts have lower inherited partisan loyalty than older, more settled constituencies and evaluate candidates primarily on concrete delivery to their economic concerns. A Democratic candidate who is ideologically aligned with the district's progressive lean but disconnected from the specific mechanisms of housing affordability, education debt relief, and healthcare access can lose support to a challenger who speaks to those material concerns more directly. Partisan identity is a starting point, not a destination, in this constituency." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "NH-HD-Strafford-10: Elite Education & Youth Density in a Swept Democratic Stronghold — Consolidate Young Professionals While Managing the 89% Margin.",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "NH-HD-Strafford-10 is a demographically exceptional district: 77.8% college-educated adults, $126,658 median household income, and a 20.7-year median age that signals a constituency dominated by university students, recent graduates, and young professionals rather than a traditional homeowner base. The 34.8% renter rate — substantially elevated for New Hampshire — confirms this profile: this is a district where transience, educational attainment, and early-career economic positioning are the dominant demographic facts. The 2024 Democratic sweep of all four at-large seats with 89% of the vote is not a surprise given this constituency's profile, but it also represents a floor risk rather than a durable ceiling. Young professionals and university-connected voters evaluate candidates on concrete delivery — housing affordability, student debt policy, healthcare access, and whether the state is supporting or extracting from their economic mobility. A Democratic incumbent who consolidates this seat without engaging the specific economic anxieties of a 77.8% college-educated, $126k-income, 34.8%-renter constituency is vulnerable to a challenger who speaks directly to those concerns with specificity.",
          "The strategic vulnerability is not partisan but demographic: in a 4-seat at-large district, the candidate who finishes outside the top 4 loses, regardless of margin. At 89% Democratic, this seat appears safe, but that aggregate number masks the coalition's composition. Young professionals in high-income, high-education districts often show strong ticket-splitting behavior when they perceive that specific candidates are not addressing their material concerns — housing costs relative to income, education debt burden, healthcare affordability, and whether local government is facilitating or obstructing their economic transition to stability. A Democratic candidate who is ideologically aligned with the constituency but operationally disconnected from the mechanisms by which young professionals actually build economic security — housing supply, student loan policy, healthcare network capacity — can underperform relative to the district's partisan lean and risk losing a top-4 finish. The path to victory is not to assume the 89% holds; it is to understand what the 77.8% college-educated, majority-renter, youngest-median-age constituency actually needs and demonstrate concrete delivery on those needs."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Lead with housing affordability as a primary economic security issue: in a $126,658-income, 34.8%-renter district with a 20.7-year median age, young professionals are evaluating whether they can transition from renting to ownership and whether the state is enabling or obstructing that transition; zoning reform, housing supply, and anti-speculation policy are concrete deliverables that resonate with this constituency far more than abstract rhetorical positioning.",
          "Engage young professionals on education debt and healthcare access as linked economic mobility concerns: with 77.8% college-educated adults, this constituency has direct lived experience with education costs and is evaluating whether state policy is helping or hurting their pathway to economic stability; student loan forgiveness frameworks, healthcare affordability for young adults, and workforce development that aligns education to employment are the material governance issues.",
          "Recognize the 34.8% renter rate as a signal of transience and non-traditional household formation: this is not a district of stable families and multi-generational homeowners; it is a district of students, young professionals, early-career couples, and others whose housing needs and civic engagement patterns are fundamentally different from traditional suburban or rural constituencies; policy and messaging must reflect this reality rather than defaulting to family-centered framing.",
          "Do not assume the 89% margin is stable or durable: a 4-seat at-large district requires top-4 finish, and a candidate who is ideologically aligned with the constituency but operationally disconnected from its actual economic concerns can underperform relative to partisan lean and lose ballot position; the path to winning is demonstrating specific, concrete delivery on the mechanisms by which young professionals build economic security."
        ]
