        issues: [
          { name: "Education Quality and Funding", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 52.7% college attainment — significantly above state average — and a median age of 41.5, this district is home to professional households with school-age children and deep investment in education quality. Property tax burden and school funding adequacy are the material governance concerns for a 83.3% homeowning constituency in a high-income area where education is a primary driver of children's future opportunity. Democrats' 64-36 sweep suggests they have successfully positioned themselves as more effective stewards of Strafford-11's schools; any Republican challenger must demonstrate concrete knowledge of this district's specific educational challenges and offer superior solutions on funding, facilities, and academic outcomes." },
          { name: "Healthcare Access and Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "Strafford County's healthcare infrastructure, primary care access, and insurance affordability are direct quality-of-life concerns for a $117,037 median income community where healthcare is not abstractly important but personally immediate. The 41.5 median age and low renter rate indicate a settled, family-oriented constituency with ongoing healthcare needs and strong preference for local healthcare delivery. Republicans cannot win this seat by opposing healthcare expansion; they must instead argue that their specific policies — competition, transparency, workforce investment — would deliver better healthcare access and affordability outcomes than current Democratic governance, with concrete evidence grounded in this district's actual healthcare market." },
          { name: "Property Tax Burden and State Fiscal Management", tag: "careful", why: "While Strafford-11's median income of $117,037 reflects substantial household wealth, property tax burden is the consistent frustration point in affluent New Hampshire communities where state education funding lags and local property taxes compensate. The 83.3% homeownership rate means this constituency feels property tax increases directly; any Republican candidate must address whether they support tax relief and how they would fund schools without it, rather than assuming that high-income homeowners are satisfied with the status quo. This is an issue where Republican messaging can resonate, but only if it offers concrete fiscal solutions rather than anti-tax slogans divorced from the actual cost of maintaining Strafford County's schools and infrastructure." },
          { name: "Ideological Anti-Government Messaging", tag: "avoid", why: "A constituency with 52.7% college education and $117,037 median income is sophisticated enough to recognize and reject simplistic anti-government rhetoric divorced from policy specifics. The 64-36 Democratic sweep suggests that Strafford-11 voters have evaluated Republicans' current anti-government positioning and concluded it does not reflect their actual needs or values. Any candidate who campaigns primarily on reducing government, cutting spending, or ideological opposition to public institutions will reinforce the perception that Republicans do not take this district's actual governance challenges seriously." },
          { name: "Treating 36% as a Winning Coalition", tag: "avoid", why: "A three-seat at-large district where Democrats swept all three seats with 64% means that Republicans captured only 36% of the vote — a result that did not even guarantee a single seat under a different electoral system. Any Republican strategy that assumes the current 36% is a sufficient base to build on, rather than recognizing it as evidence of significant constituent dissatisfaction, misconstrues the actual electoral math. Republicans must gain meaningful support from the 64% — particularly from college-educated professionals and affluent homeowners who prioritize competent governance — rather than simply consolidating existing Republican voters and hoping turnout favors them." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "Strafford-11: A High-Income, College-Educated Democratic Stronghold Where Republicans Must Compete on Competence and Governance Delivery, Not Ideology.",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "Strafford-11 is a decisively Democratic district in a state Republican legislature — a 64-36 Democratic sweep of all three at-large seats represents a district whose median household income of $117,037, college attainment of 52.7%, and homeownership rate of 83.3% do not produce the Republican outcome that those demographics might historically suggest. This is an affluent, educated, owner-occupied community in southeastern New Hampshire where the 2024 result was not an accident or an outlier but a reflection of a constituency that has explicitly chosen Democratic representation for both state and local office. The demographic profile — high income, high education, low renter rate, median age of 41.5 — describes a constituency of successful professionals and business owners who are evaluating candidates on competence, fiscal management, and whether state government is delivering on concrete quality-of-life issues. For Republicans seeking to win or defend a seat in this district in future cycles, the 64-36 margin is the starting point for understanding what went wrong, not an anomaly to be dismissed.",
          "The 64-36 Democratic result in a district this affluent and well-educated signals that the Republican Party's current positioning is not resonating with Strafford-11's constituency on the issues that matter most to them. At $117,037 median household income with 52.7% college education, this is a community of dual-income professionals, entrepreneurs, and knowledge workers who prioritize education funding, healthcare access, infrastructure quality, and tax policy that reflects their actual economic interests — not abstract ideology. The three-seat at-large format means that any candidate who finishes in the top three wins; the Democrats' sweep indicates they secured not just a majority but sufficient support across multiple constituencies to dominate all three slots. A Republican path forward in Strafford-11 requires a candidate who can speak with specificity about what Republicans would actually do for this district's schools, healthcare system, property tax burden, and regional economic development — and who can credibly argue that Democratic incumbents are underperforming on those concrete deliverables. Absent that specificity and that performance critique grounded in local facts, Republicans will continue to lose decisively in a district where affluent, educated voters have decided that Democrats are better stewards of their community's future."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Build a performance critique of incumbent Democrats on education, property tax fairness, and healthcare access — the three issues most salient to a $117k median income, 52.7% college-educated constituency — rather than ideological attacks that this district's voters actively reject.",
          "Recruit candidates with demonstrable local governance success: school board members, town managers, business leaders, or healthcare administrators who can speak with authority about what works in similar communities and why Democratic policies are underperforming on the specific metrics that matter to Strafford-11.",
          "Acknowledge that this district's high income and education are assets to the state, not targets for resentment; frame Republican priorities as tools to protect and enhance that prosperity, not to redistribute it or treat success as morally suspect.",
          "Accept that the 36% Republican result is the real floor, not an anomaly — this is a district where affluent, educated voters prefer Democratic governance on the issues they care about; winning requires both credible alternative candidates and a persuasive case that Republican solutions actually deliver better outcomes."
        ]
