          "The demographic composition — 88.77% white, with 6.15% Hispanic, 3.55% Asian, and 1.11% Black residents, with the remaining ~0.52% American Indian / Alaska Native — suggests a relatively homogeneous community where partisan sorting has become the dominant divide rather than demographic difference. At 54-46, Bixby holds the seat with a coalition of college-educated professionals, younger renters, and suburban homeowners who have moved away from Republican alignment on cultural and governance grounds. This margin is not insurmountable: any challenger who can credibly argue that the incumbent is out of touch with suburban professionals' economic concerns — cost of living, housing affordability, property tax burden, or school funding — has a viable path to competitive performance. The 31.6% renter rate is the key swing population: young professionals and families for whom housing costs and economic mobility are primary voting concerns, and who have no inherited partisan loyalty. A challenger who speaks specifically to rental-market affordability and young-professional economic opportunity in the context of a high-income district has an opening, but must do so with the specificity and policy fluency that this educated constituency demands."
