          { name: "Allowing the Election to Become a Referendum on National Democratic Cultural Politics", tag: "avoid", why: "New Hampshire is a swing state where Republicans remain genuinely competitive, and educated suburban voters nationally have shown sensitivity to how Democratic candidates are positioned on cultural issues. A 58% margin is comfortable but not fireproof; an incumbent who allows the race to become a referendum on national Democratic cultural messaging rather than state-level economic governance creates space for a Republican challenger to peel off homeowners on fiscal grounds. The vulnerability is not that the district will flip, but that a 58% margin will narrow to 52-53% if the race becomes about cultural rather than economic performance." },
          { name: "Treating a 58% Result as a Durable Structural Advantage", tag: "avoid", why: "In a swing state context, a 58% result is a mandate to perform, not a permission to coast. New Hampshire's political competitiveness and the district's economic profile mean that any incumbent who assumes this margin is durable without active constituent attention—or who neglects the 42% opposition and fails to understand what economic concerns drove it—risks a credible challenger narrowing the margin in the next cycle. The district's affluence and education level are genuine Democratic assets, but only if actively tended to with economic stewardship and constituent delivery." }
