        issues: [
          { name: "Property Tax Burden & Municipal Cost Control", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 93.7% homeownership and $100,670 median household income, NH-HD-Sullivan-2 is a homeowner-dominant community where property tax burden is the most direct connection between household finances and governance. In New Hampshire specifically, where property taxes fund local schools without a state income tax, property tax fairness and municipal spending efficiency are the primary economic anxiety for affluent homeowners. A Democratic incumbent who demonstrates concrete commitment to cost-effective municipal government and property tax sustainability holds the constituency; one who appears indifferent to cost control creates an opening for a Republican challenger positioned as a fiscal conservative." },
          { name: "Healthcare Cost & Retirement Security for Older Homeowners", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 48.5-year median age is the oldest in the dataset, indicating a constituency moving toward or already in retirement years with significant healthcare cost exposure. Affluent older adults are not immune to Medicare sustainability concerns, prescription drug pricing, long-term care cost anxiety, and the transition from work income to fixed retirement income. A Democratic candidate who leads on Medicare protection, drug pricing reform, and long-term care affordability speaks directly to the material concerns driving political volatility in this age cohort—and addresses a gap where Republican messaging often captures older voters despite Democratic policy advantages on healthcare." },
          { name: "Education Quality & Higher Education Access", tag: "careful", why: "The 43.6% college-educated adult rate is the highest in the dataset, reflecting a community that values educational attainment and property values are substantially influenced by school quality. K-12 education funding, affordability of higher education for local families, and workforce development are genuine community priorities. However, education issues in affluent suburban districts can also become flashpoints over curriculum, parental authority, and local control — requiring a Democratic incumbent to engage the education quality message with fluency while being aware that education debates in high-income communities sometimes mask other political dynamics." },
          { name: "Treating 60-40 as a Durable Majority Without Active Engagement", tag: "avoid", why: "A 60-40 Democratic result in a district with 93.7% homeownership, $100,670 median income, 48.5-year median age, and 91% white population is not historically normal and should not be treated as a durable floor. The 40% Republican share in a district with these demographics signals either specific local governance frustrations or meaningful pockets of economic anxiety among fixed-income older adults. Any incumbent who campaigns assuming the 60-40 margin is secure without actively addressing the material concerns driving that 40% opposition is vulnerable to a credible Republican challenger." },
          { name: "Progressive Economic Messaging Disconnected from Homeowner Material Interests", tag: "avoid", why: "NH-HD-Sullivan-2 is not a working-class or struggling district; it is an affluent, educated, homeowner-dominant community whose members have material stakes in property values, tax burden, and fiscal discipline. Campaign messaging that emphasizes wealth redistribution, large new spending programs without credible funding sources, or positions that appear hostile to property-owner interests will activate the Republican 40% and potentially expand it. Democratic messaging in this district must be grounded in the premise that affluent homeowners' economic security and fiscal responsibility are legitimate concerns, not obstacles to progressive governance." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "NH-HD-Sullivan-2: A Affluent, Educated, Aging Homeowner District at 60-40 Democratic, Where the 40% Opposition and Median Age 48.5 Signal Economic Anxiety Among Older Fixed-Income Households Despite High Income.",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "NH-HD-Sullivan-2 is a prosperous district on paper: $100,670 median household income, 43.6% college-educated adults, 91.35% white, and 93.7% homeownership in a state where Republican performance typically correlates with homeownership and educational attainment. Yet Palmer won with only 60% — a 20-point margin that, in a district with these demographics, should produce a substantially larger Democratic advantage if demographics alone were driving the result. The 48.5-year median age, the highest median income in the data set, and the overwhelming homeownership rate suggest a constituency of established, asset-holding adults whose material interests — property values, tax burden, healthcare costs in retirement years, and municipal service quality — are often associated with Republican messaging. The 40% Republican performance in this affluent, older, homeowner-dominant community is the central strategic puzzle: it reflects either meaningful pockets of economic anxiety among fixed-income older adults, specific local governance failures that have eroded Democratic support among the homeowner majority, or both.",
          "The 6.3% renter rate and 43.6% college attainment point to a constituency of educated professionals and established homeowners concentrated in higher-income suburbs or small cities — likely the Keene or Monadnock region of southern New Hampshire. This is a community where property tax burden, municipal service delivery (schools, infrastructure, public safety), and the sustainability of middle-class economics are the primary governance concerns. The 60-40 result is stable enough to hold, but not stable enough to assume — a 20-point margin in a district this affluent, educated, and homeowner-dominant leaves room for a credible Republican challenger to gain ground by addressing specific property-tax frustrations, municipal cost-control concerns, or demographic shifts among younger residents moving to the district. Any Democratic incumbent must maintain active constituent engagement on the material concerns of older homeowners facing rising healthcare costs and property tax pressure, not assume that high income and college attainment guarantee loyalty to the current party alignment."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Lead with property tax sustainability and municipal cost control: the 93.7% homeownership rate and $100,670 income signal a constituency whose primary governance concern is whether property taxes are rising faster than incomes, and whether municipal services (schools, infrastructure, public safety) are being delivered efficiently. Specific, credible answers on these issues are the foundation for holding affluent suburban homeowners.",
          "Address healthcare cost anxiety among the 48.5-year-old median-age constituency: older homeowners at high income are not immune to rising healthcare and long-term care costs; Medicare sustainability, prescription drug pricing, and the transition to retirement are material concerns that drive political volatility in this age cohort. Ignore these issues and a Republican challenger can make ground.",
          "Engage the 43.6% college-educated community on education funding and workforce pipeline: this district's educational attainment is a defining characteristic, and K-12 education quality and the viability of higher education are connected to property values and community identity. A Democratic incumbent should own this issue completely — it is a natural fit with the district's demographic profile.",
          "Do not assume the 60-40 margin is durable without active engagement: a 20-point Democratic lead in a district this affluent, educated, and homeowner-dominant is not a historical norm. Treat the 40% opposition as a signal that specific material concerns among older homeowners are not being adequately addressed, and conduct regular constituent engagement to identify emerging issues before they shift the margin."
        ]
