        issues: [
          { name: "Aging Homeowner Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 51.1 median age and 91.1% homeownership with $79,688 income, NH-HD-Sullivan-4 is a community of older asset-holders navigating rising fixed costs on modest incomes. Property tax management, healthcare cost control, and the viability of rural services are the primary economic security concerns for a constituency at this age and income intersection. The 61-39 Republican margin appears stable, but it rests on consolidating aging homeowners who are materially stressed — not on enthusiasm. A 39% opposition in a district this demographically homogeneous signals that some of these homeowners are evaluating alternatives." },
          { name: "Rural Service Delivery & Infrastructure", tag: "lean-into", why: "NH-HD-Sullivan-4's median household income of $79,688 is modest for New England and reflects a rural working- and middle-class constituency dependent on public infrastructure, road maintenance, and regional service networks. With 8.9% renters and 91.1% homeowners, the district's quality of life depends on what government actually delivers — pothole repair, emergency response times, broadband access, and regional economic vitality. A legislative agenda focused on these material deliverables, rather than abstract positioning, better reflects the practical concerns of homeowners evaluating whether current representation is solving problems they face." },
          { name: "Education & College Attainment in a Non-College Town", tag: "careful", why: "At 24.6% college-educated adults in a region without a major university presence, NH-HD-Sullivan-4 is an undereducated-by-New-England-standards constituency where high school completion and vocational pathways matter more than four-year degrees. This is a potential vulnerability: younger adults seeking higher education or professional advancement may relocate, and a district losing its college-bound youth is losing its future tax base and civic energy. Conversely, a legislator who invests in regional workforce development, community college partnerships, and retention of young professionals addresses both an economic development need and a latent electoral concern that the 39% opposition may be activating." },
          { name: "Treating 61% as a Stable Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 61-39 margin in a district this demographically homogeneous — 95.18% white, median age 51.1, 91.1% homeowners — should be safer than 61%. That it is not suggests underlying economic or governance frustration among the homeowner majority that partisan affiliation alone is not resolving. Any incumbent or challenger who campaigns this seat as though 61% represents comfortable ground is underestimating the specific concerns driving the 39% opposition among constituencies demographically predisposed to vote Republican." },
          { name: "Positioning as a Rural Backwater or Economically Marginal", tag: "avoid", why: "NH-HD-Sullivan-4's $79,688 median household income and 24.6% college attainment are real demographic facts, but they do not position the district as economically marginal in the Monadnock region's context — Keene and Sullivan County are working- and middle-class communities with genuine economic assets. Any candidate whose rhetoric or framing treats the district as disadvantaged or in decline will activate voter resentment among homeowners who see their communities as stable and functional. The 61% margin requires affirming the district's actual economic and civic value, not lamenting it." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "A Demographically Homogeneous, Aging Homeowner District at 61% Republican: Monadnock Region Stability Masking Underlying Economic Stress and a 39% Opposition That Reflects Practical Governance Frustration.",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "NH-HD-Sullivan-4 is a stable, older, heavily homeowner-based rural constituency in the Monadnock region of southwestern New Hampshire. The demographic profile is striking in its homogeneity: 95.18% white, 51.1 median age, 91.1% homeownership, and a median household income of $79,688 that — while solid for rural New England — masks real economic pressure on fixed-income households whose property taxes, healthcare costs, and service expectations are rising faster than incomes. The 61-39 Republican result in a district this demographically aligned with Republican constituencies nationally should, on the surface, be more comfortable. That it is not — that 39% of voters in a 95% white, 91% homeowner district are choosing Democratic representation — suggests the electoral margin is not reflecting underlying stability but rather masking genuine constituent frustration with current representation's capacity to solve material problems. In a district where homeownership is nearly universal and demographic diversity is minimal, partisan evaluation is likely driven by specific governance performance: whether roads are maintained, whether taxes are rising faster than services, whether healthcare costs are manageable, whether young people can afford to stay.",
          "The 24.6% college attainment rate is the other strategic signal in this district's profile. In New England, where educational attainment drives economic opportunity and regional competitiveness, a district at 24.6% college-educated adults is undereducated by regional standards and faces a real risk of losing younger residents to out-migration. This is not a crisis — the district has a stable, older homeowner base and is not in demographic freefall. But it is a vulnerability: if younger adults see limited local economic opportunity and educational pathways, they leave, and a district losing its college-bound youth loses its future civic energy and tax base. The 39% opposition may include younger voters evaluating whether their futures are in the Monadnock region or whether economic mobility requires relocation. A representative who addresses this by investing in regional workforce development, community college partnerships, high-speed broadband, and the infrastructure that allows younger professionals to build lives locally is addressing both an economic development challenge and a latent electoral concern. Conversely, a representative who treats the district as settled and stable — whose governance agenda does not include concrete measures to retain and develop younger cohorts — may find that 39% opposition growing as demographic change reshapes who actually lives here and what they need."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Lead with material governance delivery: road maintenance, property tax fairness, and healthcare cost management are the practical concerns of a 51.1-year-old, 91.1% homeowner constituency on $79,688 income, and where current representation succeeds or fails on these specifics directly shapes the 61-39 margin.",
          "Address the 39% opposition directly by understanding whether it is concentrated among younger adults frustrated by limited local economic opportunity, among older homeowners frustrated by rising fixed costs, or both — and offer concrete governance solutions rather than partisan affirmations.",
          "Invest specifically in workforce development and educational infrastructure: at 24.6% college attainment, the district is undereducated by New England standards, and younger adults are evaluating whether their futures are local or require out-migration; a legislator who builds partnerships with community colleges and regional employers addresses both economic development and electoral retention.",
          "Do not assume the 61% represents a comfortable hold: in a district this demographically homogeneous, a 61-39 result signals constituent frustration driving cross-partisan evaluation, and any candidate who consolidates only the existing Republican base without addressing the opposition's driving concerns will find this seat more vulnerable than the margin suggests."
        ]
