          { name: "Professional-Class Healthcare & Cost Management", tag: "lean-into", why: "Median household income at $87,597 places this district in the professional and upper-middle-class range where household healthcare costs—insurance premiums, out-of-pocket expenses, prescription drugs—are material budget items despite relative affluence. Unlike lower-income constituencies, this demographic evaluates healthcare policy on cost containment and access quality for insured families, not on coverage expansion or subsidy levels. Specific policy commitments on insurance regulation, prescription drug pricing, and preventive care access are more persuasive than generic 'healthcare' positioning." },
          { name: "Demographic Homogeneity & Potential Visibility Gaps for Minorities", tag: "careful", why: "At 85.89% white with Hispanic, Asian, and Black populations all under 4%, NH-SD-4 is a demographically homogeneous district where professional minority communities—immigrant professionals, business owners, salaried workers of color—can become invisible within broader majority-group politics. Hispanic professionals in construction, business, and services; Asian professionals in healthcare, technology, and business; and Black professionals in law, medicine, and corporate roles are present in the district but not represented in the partisan outcome as distinct constituencies. Any incumbent who treats these communities as absorbed into the broader Democratic coalition rather than as constituencies with distinct concerns around professional licensure, business regulation, and anti-discrimination enforcement risks invisible dissatisfaction that can shift turnout or support in close cycles." },
          { name: "Treating 60% as a Fragile Mandate Awaiting Reversal", tag: "avoid", why: "A 60-40 result in a district this educationally and economically homogeneous reflects genuine policy alignment with an established constituency, not a narrow margin held by marginal voters or dependent on specific local dynamics. Challenger strategy that assumes demographic change, midterm dynamics, or generic anti-incumbent sentiment will reverse this margin is misreading the strategic terrain; this district will shift only if the incumbent demonstrably fails to serve the material interests of educated, economically secure professionals who are currently aligned with Democratic priorities. Generic opposition messaging is ineffective; targeted identification of specific governance failures is required." },
          { name: "Suburban Quality-of-Life Complacency", tag: "avoid", why: "At 39.8% renters and 38.0 median age, NH-SD-4 contains both established homeowners invested in property values and younger renters seeking housing affordability and community amenities. An incumbent or challenger who assumes the homeowner majority is satisfied with suburban status quo and ignores younger renters' concerns about housing costs, neighborhood walkability, and transit access risks mobilizing an opposition constituency that can shift a 60-40 margin in a district where younger professionals and renters represent meaningful electoral share. This is a district where quality-of-life specificity—not abstract suburban preservation—is the winning positioning." }
