          { name: "Fiscal Management & Property Tax Fairness", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $84,263 median income with a 74.6% homeownership rate, property tax burden and local government efficiency are direct pocket-book concerns for the district's primary constituency. Demonstrated commitment to preventing property tax increases while maintaining service quality, auditing government spending, and holding local budgets accountable aligns with the fiscal conservatism that affluent homeowners expect. This is not abstract ideology but practical governance on an issue that affects household cash flow." },
          { name: "The 40% Opposition in an Economically Secure District", tag: "careful", why: "A 40% opposition vote in a district with substantial economic security ($84,263 income), education (29.6% college), and demographic stability (92% white) is not explained by economic desperation or demographic threat. This 40% likely reflects policy disagreements or governance performance gaps that cross-cut partisan affiliation—whether around healthcare cost control, environmental stewardship, education policy specifics, or local governance competence. Identifying which specific issues drive this opposition, rather than assuming it is a stable partisan baseline, is essential to understanding whether the 60-40 split is durable or at risk of erosion." },
          { name: "Dismissing the 40% as Ideological Opposition", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district this economically secure and educated, treating the 40% opposition as ideologically locked-in rather than persuadable on specific policy grounds will predictably underperform. College-educated, affluent voters evaluate on substance and performance, not inherited party loyalty. A candidate who campaigns as though 60% is a safe floor and neglects to address the specific policy concerns driving the 40% will find that opposition consolidating into a credible challenge in a more competitive cycle." },
          { name: "Treating Renters as Peripheral to District Strategy", tag: "avoid", why: "While 74.6% homeownership is the district's dominant tenure, the 25.4% renter rate represents roughly one-quarter of the population—a significant constituency with housing stability, affordability, and tenant protection concerns distinct from homeowners' property value focus. Renter voters, particularly younger adults entering the housing market, are often persuadable and can shift electoral dynamics when homeowner-focused strategies leave them outside the governing conversation. Specific attention to renter economic concerns (housing affordability, residential stability) prevents that group from becoming an alternative political opening." }
