          { name: "Treating Education Funding & Tax Policy as Secondary Issues", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district with 47.5% college-educated adults and a $122,145 median income, education funding quality and tax efficiency are not peripheral concerns — they are primary drivers of voter behavior. The 49% Democratic opposition in this affluent suburban context is almost certainly organized around the belief that the Republican incumbent is underinvesting in schools or managing state finances inefficiently. Any candidate who defaults to abstract conservative positioning on these issues without specifically addressing how they will fund quality schools, manage property taxes fairly, or improve state fiscal management will struggle to hold the support of the college-educated professionals who form the district's foundation." }
