          { name: "Renter Economic Mobility & Housing Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 35.8% renter rate represents roughly 2,500-3,000 renting households in a district where homeowner political infrastructure typically dominates — this population, while not a majority, is a meaningful swing constituency whose housing affordability, student debt, and early-career economic concerns may not align with incumbent messaging focused on higher-income homeowner priorities. Renters in affluent suburban districts are often young professionals or service-sector workers who have absorbed significant educational costs and face real barriers to homeownership; engaging this constituency with specific housing policy, student debt relief advocacy, and early-career wage standards directly addresses their material circumstances and prevents them from becoming available to Republican challengers offering alternative economic narratives." },
          { name: "Minority Community Visibility & Constituent Attention", tag: "careful", why: "With 3.25% Hispanic, 2.87% Black, and 2.66% Asian residents, NH-SD-15 has meaningful minority communities that, while numerically modest, represent real constituent populations with distinct economic and social concerns. In a district where the dominant political story is affluent white suburbia, minority communities can become invisible to incumbent attention — creating political vulnerability if a credible challenger offers specific engagement on immigration policy, healthcare access, or economic mobility. The Democratic baseline with these communities is strong, but it is not automatic; authentic constituent service and policy attention prevent them from becoming marginal to the incumbent's strategic calculus." },
          { name: "Treating 63% as a Durable Supermajority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 63% result in a state with competitive electoral dynamics and strong Republican infrastructure is a decisive win but not a bulletproof majority — it represents roughly 53%-47% partisan alignment in a district where approximately 15,000-17,000 voters chose the Republican candidate in the last cycle. Any incumbent who campaigns the 2026 re-election cycle as though 63% is a stable floor, rather than a margin that requires active constituent engagement and policy delivery, risks complacency at a moment when Republican challenger quality may improve or when specific policy failures create opening for competitive challenge." },
          { name: "Assuming High-Income Professional Loyalty Without Policy Specificity", tag: "avoid", why: "The 44.6% college-attainment, $94,303-income professional base is aligned with Democratic policy on many issues, but this constituency is highly engaged and evaluates on substantive policy specificity rather than partisan loyalty — they are the voters most likely to split ballots, abstain, or vote for a competitive Republican candidate if they perceive insufficient policy delivery on education quality, fiscal responsibility, or infrastructure. Messaging that relies on abstract Democratic positioning or cultural signaling rather than concrete education policy, tax policy, or property value protection will underestimate the incumbent's vulnerability with precisely the constituency most likely to remain engaged voters through an election cycle." }
