          { name: "Hispanic Voter Economic Engagement & Persuadability", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 12.51% Hispanic population in a district with 34.5% college attainment signals an educationally mobile, professionally oriented demographic cohort that votes on economic outcomes rather than inherited party loyalty. Republicans have successfully contested Hispanic voters in similar districts by emphasizing small business opportunity, wage growth, and education costs—not cultural issues—and NH-SD-20's young, professional Hispanic voters are directly susceptible to this messaging. Democratic assumption of Hispanic support without active economic engagement and Spanish-language persuasion is a pathway to margin erosion." },
          { name: "Understanding the 44% Opposition in a Young, Mobile Constituency", tag: "careful", why: "The 44% Republican result in a district with 62.7% renters, 36.4 median age, and 12.51% Hispanic population represents active opposition among young, economically mobile voters—not cultural conservatives or inherited Republicans. This opposition is most likely concentrated among renters frustrated by housing costs and wage stagnation, young professionals concerned about economic opportunity, and Hispanic voters who perceive Republican economic messaging as more credible on business and job creation. Understanding specifically whether the opposition is strongest among renters, Hispanic voters, or college-educated young adults determines whether the incumbent's re-election strategy is viable or vulnerable." },
          { name: "Treating 56% as a Safe Democratic Seat", tag: "avoid", why: "A 56-44 result in a district where nearly two-thirds of households are renters, the median age is 36, and 12.51% identify as Hispanic is not a safe Democratic margin—it is a fragile coalition of voters who could easily shift toward a credible Republican challenger offering concrete economic solutions to housing, wages, and opportunity. Democratic incumbents and candidates who treat this seat as secure and campaign primarily on national or cultural messaging will find that assumption incorrect when challenged on economic grounds." },
          { name: "Assuming Hispanic Voter Loyalty Without Active Engagement", tag: "avoid", why: "The 12.51% Hispanic population in NH-SD-20 is college-educated, economically mobile, and responsive to messaging about entrepreneurship, wages, and professional opportunity—not cultural affinity alone. A Democratic campaign that takes Hispanic support for granted, offers only cultural messaging in Spanish, or fails to contest Republicans' economic narrative about job creation and small business access, will lose persuadable Hispanic voters in this district and watch the opposition margin expand." }
