          { name: "Property Tax Fairness & Fiscal Stewardship", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $117,274 median income and 86.2% homeownership, property taxes are a direct household expense that voters can quantify and compare; state spending policies that drive mill-rate increases are the single most salient fiscal issue for this constituency. A representative who demonstrates rigorous opposition to wasteful spending, demands accountability for state-funded projects, and actively works to protect homeowners from property tax increases is credibly serving the core economic interest of a 47.1-year-old, asset-holding majority. This is not ideology—it is direct material governance that determines whether voters' largest asset appreciates or whether taxes erode their equity." },
          { name: "Education & Infrastructure Investment as Economic Foundation", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 36.1% college education and high homeownership in an affluent New England district, this constituency understands that property values depend on school quality, road maintenance, emergency response capacity, and municipal services; these are not partisan spending issues but prerequisites for the desirable community that justifies premium real estate prices. A representative who protects education funding while demanding efficiency in other areas, who maintains infrastructure without waste, and who credibly delivers on both fiscal discipline and quality-of-life maintenance is addressing the fundamental calculation that drives a 62-38 Republican margin in a high-income suburb." },
          { name: "Erosion of the 62% Margin in Affluent Suburbs", tag: "careful", why: "New England affluent suburban districts have shown vulnerability to Democratic gains in recent cycles as educated voters realign on issues beyond property tax management; if the opposition grows beyond 40%, it may signal either that fiscal performance has deteriorated or that the incumbent's positioning on environmental regulation, reproductive rights, or governance norms has shifted relative to constituent expectations. Monitoring whether the 38% opposition concentrates in specific high-education municipalities (where college-educated professionals may be responsive to different messaging) or is distributed evenly across the district is critical to understanding whether this is a stable 62-38 result or a shrinking margin." },
          { name: "Ideological Anti-Government Messaging", tag: "avoid", why: "This district's voters are not libertarian; they are affluent professionals and homeowners who expect competent government and are willing to fund it if it is well-managed. Messaging that positions government as inherently wasteful or frames public investment as a fundamental threat will alienate a constituency that relies on public schools, public roads, and municipal services and perceives them as essential to property values and quality of life. An incumbent or challenger who allows themselves to be positioned as hostile to public investment of any kind cedes credibility with the core 62% coalition." },
          { name: "Taking Voter Loyalty for Granted in a High-Income District", tag: "avoid", why: "Affluent, educated constituencies in New England have demonstrated in recent cycles that they will switch candidates if they perceive governance failure or if they conclude that a challenger offers better competence; a 62-38 result is not a license to minimize constituent engagement or assume that homeowner self-interest guarantees loyalty. Any incumbent who campaigns this seat as safely held or who neglects to demonstrate active, specific work on fiscal management and infrastructure will find that voters are capable of mobilizing for an alternative—particularly if the opposition has a credible message on fiscal discipline or if educated suburban voters have moved on cultural or governance-norm issues." }
